answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186496185}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "bbfbaab7-10b5-57a8-a26d-3c15f79b1087" :question/math-academy-id "q-175930" :question/problem "$$\n\\underbrace{\\overbrace{\\{1,2\\}\\cup \\{2\\}=\\{1,2\\}}^{P}\\,\\overbrace{\\text{or}}^{∨}\\,\\overbrace{11\\text{ is prime}}^{Q}}_{P\\,∨\\,Q}\\,\\underbrace{\\text{and}}_{∧}\\,\\underbrace{2\\text{ is a perfect square}}_{R}\n$$\n\nFor the compound statement above, we have that\n\n$\\,$ $P∨Q$ is {{field-1}},\n$\\,$ $R$ is {{field-2}},\n$\\,$ $(P∨Q)∧R$ is {{field-3}}." :question/worked-solution "First, let's recall the following facts about the \"and\" $(∧)$ and \"or\" $(∨)$ logical connectives:\n\n- The statement $A∧B$ is true if *both* $A$ and $B$ are true.\n\n- The statement $A∨B$ is true if either $A$ is true, or $B$ is true, or *both* $A$ and $B$ are true.\n\nIn our case, $P$ is true, $Q$ is true, and $R$ is false. Therefore, $P∨Q$ is true, but the result is false:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(P∨Q)∧R & ≡\\overbrace{(\\text{true}∨\\text{true})}^{\\text{true}}∧\\text{false} \\\\  & ≡\\text{true}∧\\text{false} \\\\  & ≡\\text{false}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186496309 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054643 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "49ab93ca-16ca-5e57-835f-036b59f25d63" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Truth Values of Compound Statements"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "437c25a0-ffe2-5cb2-ab86-2abe4f8570ca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186495664} {:answer/id #uuid "835af782-c34f-5315-831c-d2d73ce3599a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186495792}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "437c25a0-ffe2-5cb2-ab86-2abe4f8570ca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 175921860