" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525715} {:answer/id #uuid "d6c554c6-a35a-5d6d-8c85-3d1083908bcb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525740}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cce7551b-697b-543d-a753-797afb60888e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525715} :answer-field/id #uuid "dfb56aec-cbc6-5eb1-b7e3-d0d0324dfd8c" :answer-field/key "field-8" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525958} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "425b853c-d1b5-583d-a5ce-9de98a7e2c58" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}{x}_{2}-\\frac{1}{2}{x}_{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525373} {:answer/id #uuid "57a041f1-60e0-5295-b25b-a25100f70274" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}{x}_{2}+\\frac{1}{2}{x}_{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525432} {:answer/id #uuid "9f361199-bd83-56dd-94a9-78c6195e7a40" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}{x}_{2}-\\frac{1}{2}{x}_{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525614}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "57a041f1-60e0-5295-b25b-a25100f70274" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}{x}_{2}+\\frac{1}{2}{x}_{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525432} :answer-field/id #uuid "f2cafab0-8360-5672-9122-b5764a0bc21c" :answer-field/key "field-4" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186525972}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "03ae1482-39ea-5fad-a59a-df13068a2393" :question/math-academy-id "q-363219" :question/problem "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}4 & -2 \\\\ 2 & -1 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}],\\,Σ=[\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{30} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}],\\,V=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices shown above. Given that $A=UΣ{V}^{T}$ is a singular value decomposition of $A,$  select the correct options in the computations of the matrix $U$ below.\n\nFrom the matrix $Σ,$ we deduce the singular values ${\\sigma }_{1}=\\sqrt{30}$ and ${\\sigma }_{2}=0.$\n\nNow, we compute the $1$st column of $U.$ It is given by:\n\n|  |  | {{field-1}} |  |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${u}_{1}=\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-2}} |  |  |\n|  |  | $\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}$ |  |  |\n\nSince the second singular value is ${\\sigma }_{2}=0$, and we do not have the third singular value, we can't use the same formula to find the second and third columns of $U.$ But we know that $U$ must be orthogonal.\n\nHence, we must find vectors $u=[{x}_{1},{x}_{2},{x}_{3}]^{T}$ such that ${u}_{1}\\cdot u=0,$ which is equivalent to\n\n$$\n{{field-3}}.\n$$\n\nChoosing ${x}_{2}$ and ${x}_{3}$ as free variables, the general solution is given by:\n\n|  |  | {{field-4}} |  |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  |  | {{field-5}} |  |  |\n|  |  | ${x}_{3}$ |  |  |\n\nthat we can rewrite as:\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{2}{x}_{2}+\\frac{1}{2}{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}]=\\frac{1}{2}{x}_{2}\\underbrace{[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]}_{{b}_{1}}+\\frac{1}{2}{x}_{3}\\underbrace{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]}_{{b}_{2}}.\n$$\n\nNext, we orthogonalize ${b}_{1}$ and ${b}_{2}$ using the Gram-Schmidt process to get the corresponding orthogonal vectors ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2}.$\n\nLet ${c}_{1}={b}_{1},$ then:\n\n|  |  | {{field-6}} |  |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${c}_{2}=\\frac{2}{5}\\,\\,$ |  | $1$ |  |  |\n|  |  | {{field-7}} |  |  |\n\nFinally, normalizing orthogonal vectors ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2},$ we obtain the second and third columns of $U:$   \n\n<table cellpadding=\"0\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>\n<table cellpadding=\"0\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"right\" height=\"30px\" rowspan=\"3\" width=\"40px\">\n                ${u}_{2}=\\,\\,$\n              </td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"30px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"90px\">\n                {{field-8}}\n              </td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">$\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}$</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">\n                {{field-9}}\n              </td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n</td>\n<td>\n<table cellpadding=\"0\">\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"right\" height=\"30px\" rowspan=\"3\" width=\"40px\">\n                ${u}_{3}=\\,\\,$\n              </td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"30px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"90px\">\n                {{field-10}}\n              </td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">\n                {{field-11}}\n              </td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">$\\frac{5}{\\sqrt{30}}$</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>" :question/worked-solution "Recall that if  $A=UΣ{V}^{T}$ is a singular value decomposition of a $3\\times 2$ matrix $A,$ then\n\n- $Σ$ is a $3\\times 2$ matrix with nonnegative elements on the main diagonal and zeroes outside the main diagonal,\n\n- $U$ is an orthogonal $3\\times 3$ matrix,\n\n- $V$ is an orthogonal $2\\times 2$ matrix.\n\nFrom the matrix $Σ,$ we deduce that ${\\sigma }_{1}=\\sqrt{30}$ and ${\\sigma }_{2}=0.$\nAlso, the columns ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ of $V$ give us the right singular vectors of $A.$\n\nTherefore, we can compute the first column of $U$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1} & =\\frac{1}{{\\sigma }_{1}}A{v}_{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{30}}[\\begin{aligned}4 & -2 \\\\ 2 & -1 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{30}}[\\begin{aligned}-2\\sqrt{5} \\\\ -\\sqrt{5} \\\\ \\sqrt{5}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{6}} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nSince the second singular value is ${\\sigma }_{2}=0$, and we do not have the third singular value, we can't use the same formula to find the second and third columns of $U.$ But we know that $U$ must be orthogonal. Hence, we must find vectors \n\n$$\nu=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nsuch that ${u}_{1}\\cdot u=0$ and $‖u‖=1.$\n\nSolving ${u}_{1}\\cdot u=0,$ which is equivalent to\n\n$$\n-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{6}}{x}_{1}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}{x}_{2}+\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}}{x}_{3}=0\\,⟹\\,-2{x}_{1}-{x}_{2}+{x}_{3}=0,\n$$\n\nwe get the general solution \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{2}{x}_{2}+\\frac{1}{2}{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}]=\\frac{1}{2}{x}_{2}\\underbrace{[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]}_{{b}_{1}}+\\frac{1}{2}{x}_{3}\\underbrace{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]}_{{b}_{2}},\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{2}$ and ${x}_{3}$ are free variables. \n\nNotice that the two linearly independent solutions ${b}_{1}$ and ${b}_{2}$ are *not* mutually orthogonal. Therefore, we have to orthogonalize them using the Gram-Schmidt process. So, let \n\n$$\n{c}_{1}={b}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThen,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{c}_{2} & ={b}_{2}-\\frac{{b}_{2}\\cdot {c}_{1}}{{c}_{1}\\cdot {c}_{1}}{c}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]-\\frac{(-1)}{5}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=\\frac{2}{5}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, normalizing orthogonal vectors ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2},$ we obtain the second and third columns of $U:$   \n\n$$\n{u}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,{u}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{30}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{30}} \\\\ \\frac{5}{\\sqrt{30}}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nSo, the matrix $U$ could be the following:\n\n$$\nU=[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{6}} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{30}} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}} & \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{30}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{6}} & 0 & \\frac{5}{\\sqrt{30}}\\end{aligned}]\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186527148 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157424 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "037ff08b-3246-5eed-870b-b4951f319c96" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating a Hessian Determinant"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "376b6d35-c19b-56a0-93ca-9e64900695ac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-24" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186526607} {:answer/id #uuid "4d63a9d4-f48b-551f-a330-cb4773261398" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186526660} {:answer/id #uuid "57cd56c0-cc2d-5c47-9c19-0fd322aee1ce" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186526683} {:answer/id #uuid "7f6fbe7f-9b92-59d9-8aed-d5539feec274" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186526755} {:answer/id #uuid "d5b9d20a-a2bf-5378-921c-e265c88f998e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186526914}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7f6fbe7f-9b92-59d9-8aed-d5539feec274" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186526755} :answer-field/id #uuid "905b588f-0f57-5923-a81f-afb037b87739" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527074}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "33021c77-54f7-572b-82bd-06940f46518d" :question/math-academy-id "q-102691" :question/problem "Given that \n${f}^{′}(x,y)=[\\begin{aligned}2x{y}^{2}-2x & 2y{x}^{2}\\end{aligned}],$ \nevaluate the Hessian determinant of $f(x,y)$ at the point $(1,-1).$" :question/worked-solution "For a function $f:{R}^{n}\\to R,$ given by\n\n$$\nf:\\underbrace{[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ ⋮ \\\\ {x}_{n}\\end{aligned}]}_{x}⟶f({x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}),\n$$\n\nthe second derivative of $f$ is given by the $n\\times n$ matrix\n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(x)=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{1}∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}^{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{2}∂{x}_{n}} \\\\ ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ & ⋮ \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}_{n}^{2}}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThe Hessian determinant $H(x,y)$ is the determinant of the second derivative:\n\n$$\nH(x)=det({f}^{″}(x))\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(x,y)=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFirst, notice that since\n${f}^{′}(x,y)=[\\begin{aligned}2x{y}^{2}-2x & 2y{x}^{2}\\end{aligned}],$\nwe must have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=2x{y}^{2}-2x,\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=2y{x}^{2}.\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the second partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2x{y}^{2}-2x)=2{y}^{2}-2\\, & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2y{x}^{2})=4xy \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2x{y}^{2}-2x)=4xy\\, & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2y{x}^{2})=2{x}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the second derivative is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(x,y) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}2{y}^{2}-2 & 4xy \\\\ 4xy & 2{x}^{2}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nEvaluating the second derivative at $(1,-1),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(1,-1)=[\\begin{aligned}0 & -4 \\\\ -4 & 2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we have\n\n$$\nH(1,-1)=0\\cdot 2-(-4)\\cdot (-4)=-16.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186527179 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133737 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c28f4ffc-5168-5e71-9d77-ae313dd462ad" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Square of a Decimal"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2ff1f921-52e3-539a-b46b-8486b70c61bc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "49.29" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186526596} {:answer/id #uuid "4ef14f82-1314-55e0-aa7f-d6fa02048203" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "53" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186526663} {:answer/id #uuid "9ce61d4c-674e-501c-94f8-21794f4cd123" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "53.9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186526806} {:answer/id #uuid "dc6fe410-d7fb-5fa7-84e2-d245a133c254" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "14.29" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186526931} {:answer/id #uuid "fe7b2309-a94f-5c47-83f4-fd09d8bb04d5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "53.29" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527009}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fe7b2309-a94f-5c47-83f4-fd09d8bb04d5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "53.29" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527009} :answer-field/id #uuid "5b277ddb-c4ed-5fd4-b2dc-c14fccceaffe" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527056}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "8c77c325-2e41-51df-821c-943abffd054d" :question/math-academy-id "q-75520" :question/problem "${7.3}^{2}=$" :question/worked-solution "We need to calculate the product of $7.3$ with itself:\n\n$$\n{7.3}^{2}=(7.3)\\cdot (7.3)\n$$\n\nFirst, we ignore the decimal points and multiply as if both numbers were whole numbers:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\begin{aligned} &  &  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\begin{aligned}\\boxed{2} \\\\ \\boxed{}\\end{aligned}\\,\\,\\,\\, &  \\\\  &  &  & \\,\\,\\,\\,7\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,3\\,\\,\\,\\, \\\\ \\,\\,\\,\\,\\times \\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,7\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,3\\,\\,\\,\\, \\\\  &  & \\,\\,\\,\\,2\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,1\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,9\\,\\,\\,\\, \\\\ \\,\\,\\,\\,+\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,5\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,1\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,1\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,0\\,\\,\\,\\, \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,5\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,3\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,2\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,9\\,\\,\\,\\,\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $73\\times 73=5\\,329.$\n\nWe now count the total number of decimal places in the two factors.\n\nThere is $1$ decimal place in the first factor ($7.3$) and $1$ decimal place in the second factor ($7.3$), so their product will have $1+1=2$ decimal places.\n\nWe take our value of $5\\,329$ and insert a decimal point to make a number with $2$ decimal places:\n\n$$\n53\\,\\overset{↓}{.}\\,\\,\\,\\underbrace{29}_{2\\text{ digits}}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${7.3}^{2}=53.29\\,.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186527182 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133737 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c28f4ffc-5168-5e71-9d77-ae313dd462ad" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Square of a Decimal"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f2c06f3e-ac54-53ac-853c-0a9d19f1a9ad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "20.25" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186526982}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f2c06f3e-ac54-53ac-853c-0a9d19f1a9ad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "20.25" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186526982} :answer-field/id #uuid "80f35c7a-0c9b-50f3-b115-b30ce556b18b" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527072}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "9362856b-6dfd-5551-b35f-afef6a56607c" :question/math-academy-id "q-185205" :question/problem "Expressed as a decimal in its simplest form, $(-4.5)^{2}=${{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "We need to calculate the product of $(-4.5)$ with itself:\n\n$$\n(-4.5)^{2}=(-4.5)\\cdot (-4.5)=(4.5)\\cdot (4.5)\n$$\n\nFirst, we ignore the decimal points and multiply as if both numbers were whole numbers:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\begin{aligned} &  &  & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\begin{aligned}\\boxed{2} \\\\ \\boxed{2}\\end{aligned}\\,\\,\\,\\, &  \\\\  &  &  & \\,\\,\\,\\,4\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,5\\,\\,\\,\\, \\\\ \\,\\,\\,\\,\\times \\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,4\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,5\\,\\,\\,\\, \\\\  &  & \\,\\,\\,\\,2\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,2\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,5\\,\\,\\,\\, \\\\ \\,\\,\\,\\,+\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,1\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,8\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,0\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,0\\,\\,\\,\\, \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,2\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,0\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,2\\,\\,\\,\\, & \\,\\,\\,\\,5\\,\\,\\,\\,\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $45\\times 45=2\\,025.$\n\nWe now count the total number of decimal places in the two factors.\n\nThere is $1$ decimal place in the first factor ($4.5$) and $1$ decimal place in the second factor ($4.5$), so their product will have $1+1=2$ decimal places.\n\nWe take our value of $2\\,025$ and insert a decimal point to make a number with $2$ decimal places:\n\n$$\n20\\,\\overset{↓}{.}\\,\\,\\,\\underbrace{25}_{2\\text{ digits}}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $(-4.5)^{2}=20.25\\,.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186527933 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054683 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d418552d-d147-5736-8acd-5ba237a90e07" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the General Term for the Derivative of a Taylor Series"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "54c93b08-f179-58d2-83b0-1c656ae49846" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{6}{n+2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527467}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "54c93b08-f179-58d2-83b0-1c656ae49846" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{6}{n+2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527467} :answer-field/id #uuid "de0a5eb4-d107-5b19-9ba5-baaaf701916e" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527896}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "4358f8d2-0190-5bf2-b45f-aefdcdef214a" :question/math-academy-id "q-352556" :question/problem "The Taylor series for the function $f(x)$ about $x=0$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }(-1)^{n+1}\\frac{6}{n(n+2)}{x}^{n}.\n$$\n\nFind the series expansion of ${f}^{′}(x).$\n\n${f}^{′}(x)={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }$\n{{field-1}}$(-1)^{n+1}{x}^{n-1}$" :question/worked-solution "Writing the terms of the series explicitly, we have\n\n$$\nf(x)=2x-\\frac{3}{4}{x}^{2}+⋯+\\underbrace{(-1)^{n+1}\\frac{6}{n(n+2)}{x}^{n}}_{\\text{the }n\\text{th term}}+⋯.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating the above series term-by-term gives\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=2-\\frac{3}{2}x+⋯+\\underbrace{(-1)^{n+1}\\frac{6}{n+2}{x}^{n-1}}_{\\text{the }n\\text{th term}}+⋯.\n$$\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{6}{n+2}(-1)^{n+1}{x}^{n-1}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186527952 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054683 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d418552d-d147-5736-8acd-5ba237a90e07" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the General Term for the Derivative of a Taylor Series"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f8e12dbd-e2b8-5ddd-ae75-29b1c5d0d207" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3^n" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527791}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f8e12dbd-e2b8-5ddd-ae75-29b1c5d0d207" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3^n" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527791} :answer-field/id #uuid "ad2f7538-ba88-5617-8a32-3e7d342103d9" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527882}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "68b82aa8-1837-5666-8db7-ce28e32f59f7" :question/math-academy-id "q-347643" :question/problem "The Taylor series for the function $f(x)$ about $x=-2$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }(-1)^{n+1}\\frac{{3}^{n}}{n}(x+2)^{n}.\n$$\n\nFind the series expansion of ${f}^{′}(x).$\n\n${f}^{′}(x)={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }$\n{{field-1}}$(-1)^{n+1}:(x+2)^{n-1}$" :question/worked-solution "Writing the terms of the series explicitly, we have\n\n$$\nf(x)=3(x+2)-\\frac{9}{2}(x+2)^{2}+⋯+\\underbrace{(-1)^{n+1}\\frac{{3}^{n}}{n}(x+2)^{n}}_{\\text{the }n\\text{th term}}+⋯.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating the above series term-by-term gives\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=3-9(x+2)+⋯+\\underbrace{(-1)^{n+1}{3}^{n}(x+2)^{n-1}}_{\\text{the }n\\text{th term}}+⋯.\n$$\n\nWe can now write the Taylor series for ${f}^{′}(x)$ using the sigma notation as follows:\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{3}^{n}(-1)^{n+1}(x+2)^{n-1}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186527961 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119859 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a2926764-dfbc-5e4e-a6b0-db24a7298a64" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Coordinates of a Vector Relative To a Given Basis by Inspection"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7700fc74-62d9-5c43-a14e-3089802b8f61" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527530} {:answer/id #uuid "9d1c50f8-8c07-5027-b319-7781b3d31cfe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527631} {:answer/id #uuid "afa06ac1-854a-5acd-a258-9948876f2387" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527668} {:answer/id #uuid "d197d8cb-a53e-5fcb-9f95-86830c4ab9d9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527731} {:answer/id #uuid "e51a43de-aaa3-5ca0-b2ff-dd23d8bf03d4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\notin \\text{Span}\\{{b}_{1},{b}_{2}\\}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527758}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7700fc74-62d9-5c43-a14e-3089802b8f61" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527530} :answer-field/id #uuid "7bb29f16-8d05-55d0-ab1d-19d482085ecc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527857}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "7c3f2557-93da-5d6b-97fe-29c0805f0db0" :question/math-academy-id "q-75975" :question/problem "Find the coordinates of the vector $x$ relative to the basis $B=\\{{b}_{1},{b}_{2}\\},$ where\n\n$$\n{b}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,{b}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,x=[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 6 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nIf $[x]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}]$ then what is the value of ${x}_{2}?$" :question/worked-solution "Remember, the order of the vectors in a basis is important.\n\nNotice that \n\n$$\n\\underbrace{[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 6 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]}_{x}=\\underbrace{(\\,6\\,)}_{{x}_{1}}\\cdot \\underbrace{[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]}_{{b}_{1}}+\\underbrace{(\\,-3\\,)}_{{x}_{2}}\\cdot \\underbrace{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]}_{{b}_{2}}\n$$\n\nwhich means that\n\n$$\nx=6\\cdot {b}_{1}+(-3)\\cdot {b}_{2}\n$$\n\nand therefore\n\n$$\n[x]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, we have that ${x}_{2}=-3.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186527999 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054683 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d418552d-d147-5736-8acd-5ba237a90e07" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the General Term for the Derivative of a Taylor Series"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "553c551b-959c-5820-87fb-2b069710efc1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{a}_{n}=\\frac{{x}^{2n}}{2n+1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527468} {:answer/id #uuid "5a5063d3-43ea-5662-b377-57fe02f5d57c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{a}_{n}=\\frac{(n+1){x}^{2n}}{n}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527480} {:answer/id #uuid "9700df67-9d0d-5e87-9766-a6afb911fb23" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{a}_{n}=\\frac{(2n+1){x}^{2n+1}}{n}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527610} {:answer/id #uuid "ae4aa833-ad3f-5510-ae92-9d1e136d42f1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{a}_{n}=\\frac{(2n+1){x}^{2n}}{n}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527664} {:answer/id #uuid "f07d0cab-5716-55ac-bbae-1ac7c49f272f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{a}_{n}=\\frac{(n+1){x}^{2n+1}}{n}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527776}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ae4aa833-ad3f-5510-ae92-9d1e136d42f1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{a}_{n}=\\frac{(2n+1){x}^{2n}}{n}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527664} :answer-field/id #uuid "e9a2bcd2-2dd5-56d9-bb97-fed17319b128" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527900}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "d825b7e3-8340-5978-bde4-a68ed2d3d297" :question/math-academy-id "q-126164" :question/problem "The Taylor series for the function $f(x)$ about $x=0$ is given by \n\n$$\nf(x)={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{{x}^{2n+1}}{n}.\n$$\n\nFind ${a}_{n},$ given that\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Writing the terms of the series explicitly, we have\n\n$$\nf(x)={x}^{3}+\\frac{{x}^{5}}{2}+⋯+\\underbrace{\\frac{{x}^{2n+1}}{n}}_{\\text{the }n\\text{th term}}+⋯.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating the above series term-by-term gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =3{x}^{2}+\\frac{5{x}^{4}}{2}+⋯+\\underbrace{\\frac{(2n+1){x}^{2n}}{n}}_{\\text{the }n\\text{th term}}+⋯.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now write the Taylor series for ${f}^{′}(x)$ using the sigma notation as follows:\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(2n+1){x}^{2n}}{n}\n$$\n\nComparing the series above with the given series, we can deduce that\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}=\\frac{(2n+1){x}^{2n}}{n}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186528007 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186054683 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d418552d-d147-5736-8acd-5ba237a90e07" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the General Term for the Derivative of a Taylor Series"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "98d838ad-9b6a-5fe9-993f-42b82cce0af8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{n}{3n+4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527620}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "98d838ad-9b6a-5fe9-993f-42b82cce0af8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{n}{3n+4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527620} :answer-field/id #uuid "e36bb404-de5f-555e-8e97-3e924df8257e" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527897}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e8579e15-923e-57b1-875d-4092d7ca1f68" :question/math-academy-id "q-352513" :question/problem "The Taylor series for the function $f(x)$ about $x=2$ is given by \n\n$$\nf(x)={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(-1)^{n+1}(x-2)^{n}}{3n+4}.\n$$\n\nFind the series expansion of ${f}^{′}(x).$\n\n${f}^{′}(x)={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }$\n{{field-1}}\n$(-1)^{n+1}(x-2)^{n-1}$" :question/worked-solution "Writing the terms of the series explicitly, we have\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{x-2}{7}-\\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{10}+⋯+\\underbrace{\\frac{(-1)^{n+1}(x-2)^{n}}{3n+4}}_{\\text{the }n\\text{th term}}+⋯.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating the above series term-by-term gives\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=\\frac{1}{7}-\\frac{2(x-2)}{10}+⋯+\\underbrace{\\frac{(-1)^{n+1}n(x-2)^{n-1}}{3n+4}}_{\\text{the }n\\text{th term}}+⋯.\n$$\n\nWe can now write the Taylor series for ${f}^{′}(x)$ using the sigma notation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{n(-1)^{n+1}(x-2)^{n-1}}{3n+4} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{n}{3n+4}(-1)^{n+1}(x-2)^{n-1}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186530453 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083284 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "367fe9fb-9d92-5105-9959-f3861994aa3c" :knowledge-point/title "Expressing a Repeating Decimal as an Infinite Geometric Series"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5f9e5a1d-3f4a-5463-86bd-0a7d49d5ac4a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "300" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529810}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5f9e5a1d-3f4a-5463-86bd-0a7d49d5ac4a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "300" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529810} :answer-field/id #uuid "042655a5-af48-5ad5-8a8e-790078669a6e" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186530217} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9ffdcfd8-bb52-5ed3-b6b0-c9aba03c3e85" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{100}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529976}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9ffdcfd8-bb52-5ed3-b6b0-c9aba03c3e85" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{100}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529976} :answer-field/id #uuid "34435d1f-0b9d-51d1-a200-90903e9fb178" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186530260}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "4eef4f5e-6db7-5b25-85cf-e831027c6756" :question/math-academy-id "q-351710" :question/problem "Express $3.\\overset{―}{03}$ as a geometric series.\n\n$3.\\overset{―}{03}={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }$\n{{field-1}}$($\n{{field-2}}$)^{n}$" :question/worked-solution "The given repeating decimal can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3.\\overset{―}{03} & =3.030\\,303… \\\\  & =3+0.03+0.000\\,3+0.000\\,003+… \\\\  & =\\underbrace{3+\\frac{3}{100}+\\frac{3}{10\\,000}+\\frac{3}{1\\,000\\,000}+⋯.}_{\\text{geometric series}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe see that we have a geometric series where the first term is ${a}_{1}=3$ and the common ratio is $\\frac{1}{100}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3.\\overset{―}{03} & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{1}{r}^{n-1} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }3{(\\frac{1}{100})}^{n-1} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }300(\\frac{1}{100}){(\\frac{1}{100})}^{n-1} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }300{(\\frac{1}{100})}^{n}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186530477 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083284 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "367fe9fb-9d92-5105-9959-f3861994aa3c" :knowledge-point/title "Expressing a Repeating Decimal as an Infinite Geometric Series"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "475de52c-5657-5af5-ae34-ca7e754e0266" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }0.28{(\\frac{1}{10})}^{n}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529746} {:answer/id #uuid "54ebaf0b-0832-5b0f-abdf-3dea0a1bc5f4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }28{(\\frac{1}{1\\,000})}^{n}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529783} {:answer/id #uuid "55e74a14-47f8-5c9a-8794-7907bf68812c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }2.8{(\\frac{1}{100})}^{n}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529787} {:answer/id #uuid "934ac02a-2aad-58b3-8ca6-9b0482520b23" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }28{(\\frac{1}{100})}^{n}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529939} {:answer/id #uuid "fdad61af-1c9c-5960-9c78-925623f0b799" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }2.8{(\\frac{1}{10})}^{n}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186530210}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "55e74a14-47f8-5c9a-8794-7907bf68812c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }2.8{(\\frac{1}{100})}^{n}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529787} :answer-field/id #uuid "ef71e6d7-8cd0-5dfb-9642-a9a48b08e037" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186530396}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "72d8f071-152f-5433-92b1-acd921a8a499" :question/math-academy-id "q-5403" :question/problem "$0.0\\overset{―}{28}=$" :question/worked-solution "The given repeating decimal can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0.0\\overset{―}{28} & =0.028\\,282\\,8...… \\\\  & =0.028+0.000\\,28+0.000\\,002\\,8+… \\\\  & =\\underbrace{\\frac{28}{1\\,000}+\\frac{28}{100\\,000}+\\frac{28}{10\\,000\\,000}+⋯.}_{\\text{geometric series}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe see that we have a geometric series where the first term ${a}_{1}=\\frac{28}{1\\,000}$ and the common ratio is $r=\\frac{1}{100}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0.0\\overset{―}{28} & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{1}{r}^{n-1} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{28}{1\\,000}{(\\frac{1}{100})}^{n-1} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{28}{10}(\\frac{1}{100}){(\\frac{1}{100})}^{n-1} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }2.8{(\\frac{1}{100})}^{n}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186530485 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083284 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "367fe9fb-9d92-5105-9959-f3861994aa3c" :knowledge-point/title "Expressing a Repeating Decimal as an Infinite Geometric Series"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4501e66c-e936-5828-bdbf-7fc69696d45b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }75{(\\frac{1}{1\\,000})}^{n}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529741} {:answer/id #uuid "4f2423a1-6aa1-554e-9ac3-fa0e7e8a1303" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }75{(\\frac{1}{100})}^{n}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529770} {:answer/id #uuid "7385f523-8305-5d93-b1b7-44b6255bb94a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{75}{100}{(\\frac{1}{1\\,000})}^{n}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529875} {:answer/id #uuid "c4d8547c-bc81-5c60-b77d-f95007fe4f70" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{75}{10}{(\\frac{1}{100})}^{n}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186530072} {:answer/id #uuid "c6d13e37-3749-55ae-8490-04faa1758e3c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{75}{100}{(\\frac{1}{100})}^{n}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186530079}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4f2423a1-6aa1-554e-9ac3-fa0e7e8a1303" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }75{(\\frac{1}{100})}^{n}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529770} :answer-field/id #uuid "d8591cd6-8b70-5076-af54-15e940bc41b2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186530376}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "8a6dd2d4-a619-5ce5-a81c-14948095cb7a" :question/math-academy-id "q-14114" :question/problem "$0.\\overset{―}{75}=$" :question/worked-solution "The given repeating decimal can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0.\\overset{―}{75} & =0.757\\,575… \\\\  & =0.75+0.007\\,5+0.000\\,075+… \\\\  & =\\underbrace{\\frac{75}{100}+\\frac{75}{10\\,000}+\\frac{75}{1\\,000\\,000}+⋯.}_{\\text{geometric series}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe see that we have a geometric series where the first term ${a}_{1}=\\frac{75}{100}$ and the common ratio is $r=\\frac{1}{100}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0.\\overset{―}{75} & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{1}{r}^{n-1} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{75}{100}{(\\frac{1}{100})}^{n-1} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }75(\\frac{1}{100}){(\\frac{1}{100})}^{n-1} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }75{(\\frac{1}{100})}^{n}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186530507 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083284 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "367fe9fb-9d92-5105-9959-f3861994aa3c" :knowledge-point/title "Expressing a Repeating Decimal as an Infinite Geometric Series"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "56d7b83d-af0c-5f10-9c25-945baac62363" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "60" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529790}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "56d7b83d-af0c-5f10-9c25-945baac62363" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "60" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186529790} :answer-field/id #uuid "6374aaa6-2efd-5ae0-8e84-968f57459ea3" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186530293} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b8b955f2-cf2c-5e77-928d-7790d245b6e5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186530043}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b8b955f2-cf2c-5e77-928d-7790d245b6e5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186530043} :answer-field/id #uuid "b23ddbb5-4c49-53e8-aff0-51e2735da1f5" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186530345}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a87819e6-d20e-5138-bb0d-a3475ffdb241" :question/math-academy-id "q-351711" :question/problem "Express $6.\\overset{―}{6}$ as a geometric series.\n\n$6.\\overset{―}{6}={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }$\n{{field-1}}$($\n{{field-2}}$)^{n}$" :question/worked-solution "The given repeating decimal can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6.\\overset{―}{6} & =6.666… \\\\  & =6+0.6+0.06+0.006+… \\\\  & =\\underbrace{6+\\frac{6}{10}+\\frac{6}{100}+\\frac{6}{1\\,000}+⋯.}_{\\text{geometric series}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe see that we have a geometric series where the first term is ${a}_{1}=6$ and the common ratio is $\\frac{1}{10}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6.\\overset{―}{6} & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{1}{r}^{n-1} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }6{(\\frac{1}{10})}^{n-1} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }60(\\frac{1}{10}){(\\frac{1}{10})}^{n-1} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }60{(\\frac{1}{10})}^{n}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186531573 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166937 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7d23f2a1-55fa-5398-9016-dc6fbc55c97b" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Particular Solution of an Inhomogeneous System of Linear ODEs"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8535759b-fe73-52af-853f-f3486f41d48a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2e^{-2t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186531153}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8535759b-fe73-52af-853f-f3486f41d48a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2e^{-2t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186531153} :answer-field/id #uuid "57ab610c-28ab-5d09-83f7-d68b274b353d" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186531452} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f89927db-d31d-51e0-b063-5d1318a6634f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186531369}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f89927db-d31d-51e0-b063-5d1318a6634f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186531369} :answer-field/id #uuid "9aa355cf-d45f-5b54-bcec-6c9a1e489b0d" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186531489}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "29e3e5d7-28ea-5e8a-b874-caa92a5ebba1" :question/math-academy-id "q-336597" :question/problem "$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]x(t)+\\overbrace{[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]}^{f(t)},\\,Φ=[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{4t} & {e}^{2t} \\\\ 0 & -2{e}^{2t}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the system of differential equations and the fundamental matrix of the corresponding homogeneous system above. Using the method of variation of parameters, compute ${Φ}^{-1}\\,f(t)$ and the particular solution ${x}_{p}(t)$ of the system.\n\n<table align=\"l\" cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"40px\" rowspan=\"2\">${Φ}^{-1}\\,f(t)=\\,\\,$</td>\n<td height=\"30px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">$-{e}^{-4t}$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"40px\" rowspan=\"2\">$\\,,\\,$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"40px\" rowspan=\"2\">${x}_{p}(t)=\\,\\,$</td>\n<td height=\"30px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">$\\frac{5}{4}$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"40px\" rowspan=\"2\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td height=\"30px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-1}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td height=\"30px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-2}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>" :question/worked-solution "We'll look for a particular solution in the form\n\n$$\n{x}_{p}(t)=Φ\\,u(t)\n$$\n\nwhere $Φ$ is a fundamental matrix for the corresponding homogeneous system, $f(t)$ is the forcing column vector and\n\n$$\nu(t)=\\int {Φ}^{-1}\\,f(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nIn our case,\n\n$$\nΦ=[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{4t} & {e}^{2t} \\\\ 0 & -2{e}^{2t}\\end{aligned}],\\,f(t)=[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nSince $Φ$ is a fundamental matrix, it must be invertible. Using the formula for the inverse of a $2\\times 2$ matrix, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Φ}^{-1} & =\\frac{1}{{e}^{4t}\\cdot (-2{e}^{2t})-0\\cdot {e}^{2t}}[\\begin{aligned}-2{e}^{2t} & -{e}^{2t} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{4t}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{-{e}^{-6t}}{2}[\\begin{aligned}-2{e}^{2t} & -{e}^{2t} \\\\ 0 & {e}^{4t}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}2{e}^{-4t} & {e}^{-4t} \\\\ 0 & -{e}^{-2t}\\end{aligned}]. \\\\ {Φ}^{-1}\\,f(t) & =\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}2{e}^{-4t} & {e}^{-4t} \\\\ 0 & -{e}^{-2t}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-{e}^{-4t} \\\\ -2{e}^{-2t}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using our formula for $u(t),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u(t) & =\\int {Φ}^{-1}\\,f(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int [\\begin{aligned}-{e}^{-4t} \\\\ -2{e}^{-2t}\\end{aligned}]\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\int -{e}^{-4t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ \\int -2{e}^{-2t}\\,\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{4}{e}^{-4t} \\\\ {e}^{-2t}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our particular solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{p}(t) & =Φ\\,u(t) \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{4t} & {e}^{2t} \\\\ 0 & -2{e}^{2t}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{4}{e}^{-4t} \\\\ {e}^{-2t}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5}{4} \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186532683 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078569 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "497072ab-d582-5896-b8a2-12bf1f07c8e9" :knowledge-point/title "Expanding Sums Written in Sigma Notation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "022d05dc-a7d7-5ef0-8631-b9da9a7019a3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{1}{2}+1+\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186531939} {:answer/id #uuid "056ee954-a7cb-5e60-9627-218a7e1d895e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0+\\frac{1}{2}+1+\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186531943} {:answer/id #uuid "1aa4c19d-0c59-5793-8596-04359247472d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}+0+\\frac{1}{2}+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186531990} {:answer/id #uuid "6cae3f0d-0bf5-5bbb-9662-ef95b4236177" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}+1+\\frac{3}{2}+2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186532177} {:answer/id #uuid "7a76ca7e-a2af-55b0-a71e-732415a70234" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1-\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{1}{2}+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186532206}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "056ee954-a7cb-5e60-9627-218a7e1d895e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0+\\frac{1}{2}+1+\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186531943} :answer-field/id #uuid "d68eecca-429b-532a-9c81-1272fffd93c2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186532596}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "94b5f894-6e26-57ef-8a2f-727471817674" :question/math-academy-id "q-5392" :question/problem "Express ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{4}(\\frac{n}{2}-\\frac{1}{2})$ as a sum of four terms." :question/worked-solution "We compute the terms for $n=1,2,3,4$ and write the sum, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=1}^{4}(\\frac{n}{2}-\\frac{1}{2}) & =\\overbrace{(\\frac{(1)}{2}-\\frac{1}{2})}^{1\\text{st}}+\\overbrace{(\\frac{(2)}{2}-\\frac{1}{2})}^{2\\text{nd}}+\\overbrace{(\\frac{(3)}{2}-\\frac{1}{2})}^{3\\text{rd}}+\\overbrace{(\\frac{(4)}{2}-\\frac{1}{2})}^{4\\text{th}} \\\\  & =(\\frac{1}{2}-\\frac{1}{2})+(1-\\frac{1}{2})+(\\frac{3}{2}-\\frac{1}{2})+(2-\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =0+\\frac{1}{2}+1+\\frac{3}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186532712 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078569 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "497072ab-d582-5896-b8a2-12bf1f07c8e9" :knowledge-point/title "Expanding Sums Written in Sigma Notation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "59942ec2-f999-54cb-bde0-92e0e42fdfa9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5+2-1-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186532129} {:answer/id #uuid "66773157-73be-5aa3-9f04-c6d84f04623f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2-1-0-7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186532161} {:answer/id #uuid "bf23797f-f9d6-59bb-b91f-b58c7d8cd88e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2-1-7-10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186532344} {:answer/id #uuid "f0d9a41f-c119-59f4-a036-8cfdd1dfebbd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1-4-7-10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186532452} {:answer/id #uuid "f4c1655c-1a29-5a11-b909-bbfec3b4ef50" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2-1-4-7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186532464}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f4c1655c-1a29-5a11-b909-bbfec3b4ef50" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2-1-4-7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186532464} :answer-field/id #uuid "b447e21a-113a-5f94-9b3f-419d7454210b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186532575}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e8b1d070-4e0e-57ce-95f8-38d74f953d38" :question/math-academy-id "q-66988" :question/problem "Express ${\\sum }_{i=1}^{4}(5-3i)$ as a sum of four terms." :question/worked-solution "The sum takes integer values of $i$ from $1$ to $4.$ So, we compute the terms for $i=1,2,3,4$ and write the sum, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{4}(5-3i) & =\\overbrace{(5-3(1))}^{i\\,=\\,1}+\\overbrace{(5-3(2))}^{i\\,=\\,2}+\\overbrace{(5-3(3))}^{i\\,=\\,3}+\\overbrace{(5-3(4))}^{i\\,=\\,4} \\\\  & =(5-3)+(5-6)+(5-9)+(5-12) \\\\  & =2-1-4-7\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186534987 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068797 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "26f44f24-4cb6-5d0d-b973-8820171cfff1" :knowledge-point/title "Solving for an Unknown Given the Value of a Left Riemann Sum With Regular Step Size"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e6f68175-b7da-5e97-b323-b3ecf7706e24" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534775}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e6f68175-b7da-5e97-b323-b3ecf7706e24" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534775} :answer-field/id #uuid "f4210f50-ff89-5293-88ab-8316c3879985" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534963}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "247e80ec-6b94-56ac-94f4-78dcaa13b56f" :question/math-academy-id "q-315696" :question/problem "Maya runs a lemonade stand. The table below gives the selling rate $r(t)$ (in liters per hour) over a $6$-hour period starting at noon.\n\n| $t\\,(\\text{hours})$ | $0$ | $2$ | $4$ | $6$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $r(t)\\,(\\text{L/hour})$ | $1$ | $k$ | $5$ | $3$ |\n\nIf the left Riemann sum approximation of the total number of liters sold over the $6$-hour period (with three subintervals defined by the given points) is $28$ liters, then what is the value of $k?$ \n\n$\\,\\,k=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The area $A$ under the curve $y=r(t)$ can be approximated using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ≈(\\overbrace{r({t}_{0})+r({t}_{1})+…+r({t}_{n-1})}^{\\text{heights}})\\cdot \\,\\,\\underbrace{Δt}_{\\text{ }\\text{width}} \\\\  & =(r(0)+r(2)+r(4))\\cdot Δt.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we now set up a table with the values of $r(t)$ at each point:\n\n| $t$ | $0$ | $2$ | $4$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $r(t)$ | $1$ | $k$ | $5$ |\n\nSo, we obtain the following approximation to the area:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ≈(1+k+5)\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =(k+6)\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =2k+12\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, in the given context, the area under the curve represents the total number of liters sold by Maya. Since we are given that the left Riemann sum approximation of the total number of liters sold is $28,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2k+12 & =28 \\\\ 2k & =16 \\\\ k & =8.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186534991 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068798 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3b6fcb8a-dd99-5e4e-a324-ed8994fe4574" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether a Left Riemann Sum is an Overestimate or Underestimate"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "681e5cac-8278-59c0-9cb0-0431661360f8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "increasing" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534476} {:answer/id #uuid "b971d682-7baf-5c86-8f72-52ba7b01ea60" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "decreasing" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534679}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "681e5cac-8278-59c0-9cb0-0431661360f8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "increasing" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534476} :answer-field/id #uuid "0e022b11-7e38-5845-8ce8-d823ff176f78" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534838} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5bfeaaed-4260-554a-aed3-a5bacbddffde" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "54.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534450} {:answer/id #uuid "8e5d3818-a0f9-5df3-97cf-a289e19e25f2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "60" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534575} {:answer/id #uuid "e84c0fb2-d0da-5c46-9013-29649f607491" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "56" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534779} {:answer/id #uuid "eac4869b-ddab-5f08-9de9-9c32f40be8bb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "58" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534787}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e84c0fb2-d0da-5c46-9013-29649f607491" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "56" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534779} :answer-field/id #uuid "228f7032-0430-5206-88b5-0be9f2a8574f" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534852} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "15977689-22a7-5a1e-aa5e-b101b88ce8dd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "exact value" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534314} {:answer/id #uuid "9fe08a89-41ef-5c59-af03-7a5095196299" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "overestimate" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534625} {:answer/id #uuid "c0127cd4-ffb2-567a-9f87-a5b7ee9a3fd8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "underestimate" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534692}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c0127cd4-ffb2-567a-9f87-a5b7ee9a3fd8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "underestimate" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534692} :answer-field/id #uuid "a63a1ae7-518a-536a-919b-d5fff57d94d1" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534922}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "2a6ffc0a-296f-5d33-ab74-d79dec06ff68" :question/math-academy-id "q-346698" :question/problem "Consider the area under the curve $y=2{x}^{2}-1$ over the interval $[1,5]$ using a left Riemann sum with step size $Δx=1.$\n\nThe estimated area under the curve $y=2{x}^{2}-1$ over the interval $[1,5]$ is {{field-1}}.\nSince the function is strictly {{field-2}}\nin the interval,  the approximation is an\n{{field-3}}\nof the actual area." :question/worked-solution "![](images/67/671d5efc8abc559cf638bfbfdda76bddb0b8d5c2d9134dffbbf7eab0e2f0b25a.png)\n\nThe area $A$ can be approximated using the formula\n\n$$\nA≈(\\overbrace{f(1)+f(2)+f(3)+f(4)}^{\\text{heights}})\\cdot \\underbrace{Δx}_{\\text{width}}.\n$$\n\nSo, we now set up a table with the values of $f(x)$ at each point:\n\n| $x$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ | $4$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $1$ | $7$ | $17$ | $31$ |\n\nFinally, we obtain the following approximation for our area:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ≈(1+7+17+31)\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =56\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =56\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBecause the function is strictly $\\text{increasing}$ over the interval $[1,5],$ the rectangles reach *below* the function, which means our approximation is an $\\text{underestimate}$ of the actual area."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186535007 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068703 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0d32eba8-a168-5c13-89a8-d2188a2aab78" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Left Riemann Sum With Regular Step Size"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "185282f2-7ff6-5c6b-b9b9-e4a2049ff490" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534320} {:answer/id #uuid "1a540d1d-50f6-5433-a351-e20154df9816" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "15" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534322} {:answer/id #uuid "2241ebaf-ee5e-55f8-ae67-45d4013fe205" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534337} {:answer/id #uuid "4a2d11e1-346f-5678-87b0-f47869751fb1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534411} {:answer/id #uuid "9abf62e4-c1ec-5172-86a6-3a5ad254967f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534609}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2241ebaf-ee5e-55f8-ae67-45d4013fe205" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534337} :answer-field/id #uuid "4f5a1c82-2697-5ad0-92be-ffc88f085f93" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534878}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "4bbaa29d-7536-57fa-abdb-fc58099b0936" :question/math-academy-id "q-86288" :question/problem "The table below gives the values of a continuous function $f(x)$ over the closed interval $[1,10]$ with a regular step size. Estimate the area under the curve $y=f(x)$ over the interval $[1,10]$ using a left Riemann sum with $3$ equal subintervals.\n\n| $x$ | $1$ | $4$ | $7$ | $10$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $1$ | $2$ | $0$ | $2$ |" :question/worked-solution "The area $A$ can be approximated using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ≈(\\overbrace{f({x}_{0})+f({x}_{1})+…+f({x}_{n-1})}^{\\text{heights}})\\cdot \\,\\,\\underbrace{Δx}_{\\text{ }\\text{width}} \\\\  & =(f(1)+f(4)+f(7))\\cdot Δx.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the interval $[1,10]$ is divided into $3$ equal subintervals, the width of each subinterval is\n\n$$\nΔx=\\frac{10-1}{3}=3.\n$$\n\nSo, we now set up a table with the values of $f(x)$ at the left endpoints:\n\n| $x$ | $1$ | $4$ | $7$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $1$ | $2$ | $0$ |\n\nFinally, we obtain the following approximation for our area:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ≈(1+2+0)\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =3\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =9.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186535010 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068703 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0d32eba8-a168-5c13-89a8-d2188a2aab78" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Left Riemann Sum With Regular Step Size"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1314f4b3-9571-556b-ad75-81e6e271fd2f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "25" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534306}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1314f4b3-9571-556b-ad75-81e6e271fd2f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "25" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534306} :answer-field/id #uuid "60816e31-a460-50bd-9564-d4219818a430" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534889}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "52c6a4cd-b903-5d26-bd7b-6413b0fd0ae1" :question/math-academy-id "q-364487" :question/problem "Estimate the area $A$ under the curve $y=4{x}^{2}-1$ over the interval $[1,3]$ using a left Riemann sum with step size $Δx=0.5.$ Do not round the answer.\n\n$A≈$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Let's sketch our situation.\n\n![](images/27/275b62fbec1c1a550cb23b481ec54c1e12d3d42e70cf95cd917f1b932638add4.png)\n\nThe area $A$ can be approximated using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ≈(\\overbrace{f({x}_{0})+f({x}_{1})+…+f({x}_{n-1})}^{\\text{heights}})\\cdot \\underbrace{Δx}_{\\text{ }\\text{width}} \\\\  & =(f(1)+f(1.5)+f(2)+f(2.5))\\cdot 0.5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we now set up a table with the values of $f(x)=4{x}^{2}-1$ at each left endpoint:\n\n| $x$ | $1$ | $1.5$ | $2$ | $2.5$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $3$ | $8$ | $15$ | $24$ |\n\nFinally, we obtain the following approximation for our area:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ≈(3+8+15+24)\\cdot 0.5 \\\\  & =50\\cdot 0.5 \\\\  & =25\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186535025 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068703 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0d32eba8-a168-5c13-89a8-d2188a2aab78" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Left Riemann Sum With Regular Step Size"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9fc50b29-7baf-5a27-96d5-142128ec93ab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "65\\,\\text{mi}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534624} {:answer/id #uuid "a014c8ae-f0bd-5ff7-a1e7-9e3747de7adf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "75\\,\\text{mi}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534626} {:answer/id #uuid "b23a1ed8-832f-51a4-9037-8703bcf7ddc6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "115\\,\\text{mi}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534665} {:answer/id #uuid "d4a87ab8-97db-50f2-b37e-b52d8b7bc07a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "80\\,\\text{mi}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534741} {:answer/id #uuid "d4d6386f-3f09-5d69-8338-27e6df95af4d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "110\\,\\text{mi}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534743}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a014c8ae-f0bd-5ff7-a1e7-9e3747de7adf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "75\\,\\text{mi}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534626} :answer-field/id #uuid "6b71fa70-bd24-5e39-8659-a1858750b9fc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534892}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "725799d2-b847-5f90-a1b3-5a3cdfe95070" :question/math-academy-id "q-19890" :question/problem "A speedboat travels downstream on a river. The table below gives the speed $v(t)$, in miles per hour, of the boat at certain moments during its journey.\n\n| $t\\,(\\text{hours})$ | $0$ | $0.5$ | $1$ | $1.5$ | $2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $v(t)\\,(\\text{mi/hour})$ | $10$ | $35$ | $40$ | $65$ | $80$ |\n\nUse a left Riemann sum for $v(t)$ over the interval $[0,2]$ with four subintervals to approximate the total distance traveled by the speedboat.\n\n*Hint: The distance traveled is given by the area under the speed-time graph.*" :question/worked-solution "First, let's compute the step size $Δt,$ which is just the width of each rectangle. In this case, the endpoints of our interval are $a=0$ and $b=2,$ and there are $n=4$ rectangles, so the step size is\n\n$$\nΔt=\\frac{b-a}{n}=\\frac{2-0}{4}=0.5.\n$$\n\nThe area $A$ can be approximated using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ≈(\\overbrace{v({x}_{0})+v({t}_{1})+…+v({t}_{n-1})}^{\\text{heights}})\\cdot \\,\\,\\underbrace{Δt}_{\\text{ }\\text{width}} \\\\  & =(v(0)+v(0.5)+v(1)+v(1.5))\\cdot Δt.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we now set up a table with the values of $v(t)$ at each point:\n\n| $t$ | $0$ | $0.5$ | $1$ | $1.5$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $v(t)$ | $10$ | $35$ | $40$ | $65$ |\n\nNow, we obtain the following approximation for the area:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ≈(10+35+40+65)\\cdot 0.5 \\\\  & =150\\cdot 0.5 \\\\  & =75\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nFinally, in the given context, the area under the curve represents the total distance traveled by the speedboat. So, the distance is $75\\,\\text{mi}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186535030 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068720 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5df52cb5-75b1-58d9-8535-07c1bb87be34" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Length of a Segment"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0eec1ef8-9fdc-5230-9417-7755a8d0b87e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534296} {:answer/id #uuid "0f8e6dec-43af-5f80-ab1e-3eef631db8bf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534300} {:answer/id #uuid "43e972e7-cf56-5b6c-b604-0698d249e7cb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534393} {:answer/id #uuid "9dda4d19-a039-5a94-a7cf-7a1838c89803" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\sqrt{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534617} {:answer/id #uuid "e6e5abdb-b91e-58f8-a040-8654f0c602a4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534774}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0eec1ef8-9fdc-5230-9417-7755a8d0b87e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534296} :answer-field/id #uuid "50de03fc-f361-5d04-9b42-75d709112b35" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534880}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "7b6428c3-ddc3-50c7-8b44-d4c45bc81ecd" :question/math-academy-id "q-3720" :question/problem "![](images/6a/6a225129d0879af950e935d794c09f3b479434154757a643c29c44a9d9e981e2.png)\n\nWhat is the length of $\\overset{―}{DE}?$" :question/worked-solution "From the figure, we note that the points $D$ and $E$ have coordinates $\\underbrace{(-4,0)}_{\\text{(}{x}_{1}\\text{,}\\,{y}_{1})}$ and $\\underbrace{(0,3)}_{\\text{(}{x}_{2}\\text{,}\\,{y}_{2})},$  respectively.\n\nTo find the length of $\\overset{―}{DE},$ we use the distance formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}DE & =\\sqrt{({x}_{2}-{x}_{1})^{2}+({y}_{2}-{y}_{1})^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(0-(-4))^{2}+(3-0)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(4)^{2}+(3)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{16+9} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{25} \\\\  & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186535034 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068798 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3b6fcb8a-dd99-5e4e-a324-ed8994fe4574" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether a Left Riemann Sum is an Overestimate or Underestimate"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "609bcbfa-3548-57cf-bcc3-93d490bcb531" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534460} {:answer/id #uuid "7fef99b6-9e65-5243-850a-68688028042a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2.25" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534542} {:answer/id #uuid "df771a6e-1232-5f6a-84e1-694037e34463" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534762} {:answer/id #uuid "f9fddebf-ef73-5795-a7f6-950173f0b125" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2.75" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534818}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "df771a6e-1232-5f6a-84e1-694037e34463" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534762} :answer-field/id #uuid "74bd8deb-7d74-51bf-8a85-e4c7b6829053" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534895} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a769d530-b6a1-5708-87df-5205a70c420b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "underestimate" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534639} {:answer/id #uuid "b24be7fe-23bc-5f34-826f-f1abc2a0549a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "overestimate" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534666} {:answer/id #uuid "c7c006f8-9c2c-5582-91b2-eae40baaa5cc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "exact value" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534715}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a769d530-b6a1-5708-87df-5205a70c420b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "underestimate" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534639} :answer-field/id #uuid "b0827ea4-90d5-5a4c-b262-f414a3213a8d" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534932} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "09afc441-68cd-5e99-bd07-fc18988c6c92" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "increasing" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534280} {:answer/id #uuid "1cfdebaf-418b-55bc-ae86-c86ee148325a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "decreasing" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534325}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "09afc441-68cd-5e99-bd07-fc18988c6c92" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "increasing" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534280} :answer-field/id #uuid "e1d1e6f2-d3d9-54c9-8ea2-99f5774ef677" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534956}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "7e9c9afd-571f-54f0-b5de-d6a5e5729d5e" :question/math-academy-id "q-346697" :question/problem "Consider the area under the curve $y=4x+1$ over the interval $[0,1]$ using a left Riemann sum with step size $Δx=0.25.$\n\nThe estimated area under the curve $y=4x+1$ over the interval $[0,1]$ is {{field-1}}.\nSince the function is strictly {{field-2}}\nin the interval,  the approximation is an\n{{field-3}}\nof the actual area." :question/worked-solution "![](images/e8/e8cd8966d69260c77685d349c97eceb1545686d347f6cdb827ad470880797bf5.png)\n\nThe area $A$ can be approximated using the formula\n\n$$\nA≈(\\overbrace{f(0)+f(0.25)+f(0.5)+f(0.75)}^{\\text{heights}})\\cdot \\underbrace{Δx}_{\\text{width}}.\n$$\n\nSo, we now set up a table with the values of $f(x)$ at each point:\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $0.25$ | $0.5$ | $0.75$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ | $4$ |\n\nFinally, we obtain the following approximation for our area:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ≈(1+2+3+4)\\cdot 0.25 \\\\  & =10\\cdot 0.25 \\\\  & =2.5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBecause the function is strictly $\\text{increasing}$ over the interval $[0,1],$ the rectangles reach *below* the function, which means our approximation is an $\\text{underestimate}$ of the actual area."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186535036 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068797 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "26f44f24-4cb6-5d0d-b973-8820171cfff1" :knowledge-point/title "Solving for an Unknown Given the Value of a Left Riemann Sum With Regular Step Size"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "228eec43-938a-5953-916c-999c2cbbaa37" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534339} {:answer/id #uuid "6776dc08-9fdf-51fd-996a-bfab15c3331d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534474} {:answer/id #uuid "89cc52d0-7d13-5b28-b988-28ce44b1ddc9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534564} {:answer/id #uuid "9d78173e-a4fa-5da6-a57c-b9e952b87134" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534616} {:answer/id #uuid "a159d0cd-4dba-5b36-b32e-909f05583e6d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534628}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "89cc52d0-7d13-5b28-b988-28ce44b1ddc9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534564} :answer-field/id #uuid "e817f9ef-3f03-51be-bb7b-0bc236498520" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534958}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "84844930-f95f-5f3a-8037-4adc201a50fc" :question/math-academy-id "q-86298" :question/problem "The following table shows the rate of change of a city's temperature over a $10$-hour period, starting at $2\\,\\text{a.m}.$\n\n| $t\\,(\\text{h})$ | $2$ | $4$ | $6$ | $8$ | $10$ | $12$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $T\\,{(}^{∘}\\text{C}/\\text{h})$ | $1$ | $k$ | $4$ | $2$ | $3$ | $1$ |\n\nIf the left Riemann sum approximation of the total change in the temperature from $2\\,\\text{a.m}$ to $12\\,\\text{p.m}$ has a value of $26{\\,}^{∘}\\text{C},$ what is the value of $k?$" :question/worked-solution "The area $A$ under the curve $y=T(t)$ can be approximated using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ≈(\\overbrace{T({t}_{0})+T({t}_{1})+…+T({t}_{n-1})}^{\\text{heights}})\\cdot \\,\\,\\underbrace{Δt}_{\\text{ }\\text{width}} \\\\  & =(T(2)+T(4)+T(6)+T(8)+T(10))\\cdot Δt.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we now set up a table with the values of $T(t)$ at each point:\n\n| $t$ | $2$ | $4$ | $6$ | $8$ | $10$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $T(t)$ | $1$ | $k$ | $4$ | $2$ | $3$ |\n\nSo, we obtain the following approximation to the area:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ≈(1+k+4+2+3)\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =2(k+10) \\\\  & =2k+20\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, in the given context, the area under the curve represents the total change in the temperature from $2\\,\\text{a.m}$ to $12\\,\\text{p.m}.$ Since we are given that the left Riemann sum approximation of the total change in the temperature is $26{\\,}^{∘}\\text{C},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2k+20 & =26 \\\\ 2k & =6 \\\\ k & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186535040 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068798 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3b6fcb8a-dd99-5e4e-a324-ed8994fe4574" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether a Left Riemann Sum is an Overestimate or Underestimate"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "515320ab-5f99-5b9e-8e87-3bb603a6ba82" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$3.75,$ overestimate" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534426} {:answer/id #uuid "5ee58283-1522-5f63-854d-16362a3b4b0c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$3.75,$ underestimate" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534455} {:answer/id #uuid "83da73b0-48f3-586d-85b3-eb18d7a17400" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$4.57,$ overestimate" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534550} {:answer/id #uuid "da4e4c5a-7f58-5eea-ab10-1c79a44758d0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$5.73,$ overestimate" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534756} {:answer/id #uuid "f2fb0583-ff57-5c40-ac23-4c564c2513d2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$5.73,$ underestimate" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534804}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5ee58283-1522-5f63-854d-16362a3b4b0c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$3.75,$ underestimate" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534455} :answer-field/id #uuid "1c4ed498-d752-509d-b505-ce28b302f4d8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534847}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "8bc09582-a4d5-5866-bf69-244d81d8d845" :question/math-academy-id "q-74503" :question/problem "Estimate the area under the curve $y={x}^{2}-2x+2$ over the interval $[1,3]$ using a left Riemann sum with step size $Δx=0.5.$ Is this an overestimate or an underestimate?" :question/worked-solution "![](images/2c/2cd664908ea218fecf5a33ea3312bd883f5495d64fc0da8387632a7d2e17c26a.png)\n\nThe area $A$ can be approximated using the formula\n\n$$\nA≈(\\overbrace{f(1)+f(1.5)+f(2)+f(2.5)}^{\\text{heights}})\\cdot \\underbrace{Δx}_{\\text{width}}.\n$$\n\nSo, we now set up a table with the values of $f(x)$ at each point:\n\n| $x$ | $1$ | $1.5$ | $2$ | $2.5$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $1$ | $1.25$ | $2$ | $3.25$ |\n\nFinally, we obtain the following approximation for our area:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ≈(1+1.25+2+3.25)\\cdot 0.5 \\\\  & =7.5\\cdot 0.5 \\\\  & =3.75\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBecause the function is *strictly increasing* over the interval $[1,3],$ the rectangles reach *below* the function, which means our approximation is an *underestimate* of the actual area."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186535046 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068797 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "26f44f24-4cb6-5d0d-b973-8820171cfff1" :knowledge-point/title "Solving for an Unknown Given the Value of a Left Riemann Sum With Regular Step Size"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "556a6e29-9fdc-550d-b85c-3606a717e3f5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534436}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "556a6e29-9fdc-550d-b85c-3606a717e3f5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534436} :answer-field/id #uuid "094a080c-b2c0-5da9-a43a-31f0cd0dae34" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534833}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "9be6577c-8d1d-5823-8ae8-eea53999e09f" :question/math-academy-id "q-315694" :question/problem "The table below gives the values of a continuous function $f(x)$ over the closed interval $[2,8]$ with a regular step size.\n\n| $x$ | $2$ | $4$ | $6$ | $8$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $-3$ | $5$ | $k$ | $1$ |\n\nIf the left Riemann sum approximation of the area under the curve $y=f(x)$ with $3$ subintervals defined by the given points has a value of $18$, then what must be the value of $k?$ \n\n$\\,\\,k=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The area $A$ can be approximated using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ≈(\\overbrace{f({x}_{0})+f({x}_{1})+f({x}_{2})}^{\\text{heights}})\\cdot \\,\\,\\underbrace{Δx}_{\\text{ }\\text{width}} \\\\  & =(f(2)+f(4)+f(6))\\cdot Δx.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the points are $2,4,6,8$ with three subintervals, the step size is\n\n$$\nΔx=\\frac{8-2}{3}=2.\n$$\n\nSo, we now set up a table with the values of $f(x)$ at each left-endpoint:\n\n| $x$ | $2$ | $4$ | $6$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $-3$ | $5$ | $k$ |\n\nSo, we obtain the following approximation to the area:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ≈((-3)+5+k)\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =(2+k)\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =4+2k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since we are given that $A=18$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4+2k & =18 \\\\ 2k & =14 \\\\ k & =7.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186535059 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068703 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0d32eba8-a168-5c13-89a8-d2188a2aab78" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Left Riemann Sum With Regular Step Size"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "210cb1e7-4d4f-521d-94ea-b92ddb31ec30" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5.35" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534333} {:answer/id #uuid "729df441-7198-586b-a2e8-ab6ac0c01a83" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3.75" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534507} {:answer/id #uuid "a1f94a57-31fa-5cc6-ae1d-146b8abaa792" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7.53" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534630} {:answer/id #uuid "e7dec5bb-a6e8-5021-8f8f-827f57c20855" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7.35" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534778} {:answer/id #uuid "efdc7fbe-3706-5051-a3b0-a9413406ae28" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3.57" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534800}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "729df441-7198-586b-a2e8-ab6ac0c01a83" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3.75" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534507} :answer-field/id #uuid "aa9732c7-7fe4-5d54-92ce-d54efeda2989" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534925}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "b62ef58e-043d-550b-83fb-b95a07d27915" :question/math-academy-id "q-74490" :question/problem "Estimate the area under the curve $y=3x-{x}^{2}$ over the interval $[0.5,2.5]$ using a left Riemann sum with step size $Δx=0.5.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's sketch our situation.\n\n![](images/0e/0e3f499cbc36c9dca7e759b67a8fd9e94959039f3403832e1d60b9ed598c44dd.png)\n\nThe area $A$ can be approximated using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ≈(\\overbrace{f({x}_{0})+f({x}_{1})+…+f({x}_{n-1})}^{\\text{heights}})\\cdot \\,\\,\\underbrace{Δx}_{\\text{ }\\text{width}} \\\\  & =(f(0.5)+f(1)+f(1.5)+f(2))\\cdot Δx.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we now set up a table with the values of $f(x)$ at each point:\n\n| $x$ | $0.5$ | $1$ | $1.5$ | $2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $1.25$ | $2$ | $2.25$ | $2$ |\n\nFinally, we obtain the following approximation for our area:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ≈(1.25+2+2.25+2)\\cdot 0.5 \\\\  & =7.5\\cdot 0.5 \\\\  & =3.75\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186535060 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068798 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3b6fcb8a-dd99-5e4e-a324-ed8994fe4574" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether a Left Riemann Sum is an Overestimate or Underestimate"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2c60a466-a39a-5a5b-bb84-5d4cb7876254" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$2.75,$ underestimate" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534355} {:answer/id #uuid "8c1b0e03-2b27-5442-9fcf-fd518b599444" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$4.75,$ underestimate" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534568} {:answer/id #uuid "9f63f2ef-7e04-5845-a994-8b3a76a5610d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$5.75,$ overestimate" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534622} {:answer/id #uuid "a35d4692-5d9f-53e3-93e2-8277320977bf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$2.75,$ overestimate" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534635} {:answer/id #uuid "da4041b1-902c-5100-90dd-0f72405fae3b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$4.75,$ overestimate" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534755}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "da4041b1-902c-5100-90dd-0f72405fae3b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$4.75,$ overestimate" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534755} :answer-field/id #uuid "d4c68885-5ff0-588b-a710-c9a4d588c9db" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534949}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "b8274948-1d4b-57b0-9024-c09c00401038" :question/math-academy-id "q-115695" :question/problem "Estimate the area under the curve $y=-{x}^{2}+3x+1$ over the interval $[1.5,3.5]$ using a left Riemann sum with step size $Δx=0.5.$ Is this an overestimate or an underestimate?" :question/worked-solution "![](images/ad/ad1e85721eb2142f5cad422530aeed86c0919f34261d19e11e9f056cf355b387.png)\n\nThe area $A$ can be approximated using the formula\n\n$$\nA≈(\\overbrace{f(1.5)+f(2)+f(2.5)+f(3)}^{\\text{heights}})\\cdot \\underbrace{Δx}_{\\text{width}}.\n$$\n\nSo, we now set up a table with the values of $f(x)$ at each point:\n\n| $x$ | $1.5$ | $2$ | $2.5$ | $3$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $3.25$ | $3$ | $2.25$ | $1$ |\n\nFinally, we obtain the following approximation for our area:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ≈(3.25+3+2.25+1)\\cdot 0.5 \\\\  & =9.5\\cdot 0.5 \\\\  & =4.75\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBecause the function is *strictly decreasing* over the interval $[1.5,3.5],$ the rectangles reach *above* the function, which means our approximation is an *overestimate* of the actual area."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186535071 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068703 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0d32eba8-a168-5c13-89a8-d2188a2aab78" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Left Riemann Sum With Regular Step Size"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0da9d2c8-f87f-52ab-bc1e-affb04efcc9b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$400$ liters" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534291} {:answer/id #uuid "0ebdf27a-4c72-5cb8-aa57-5a63e946d3bc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$600$ liters" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534294} {:answer/id #uuid "3caddbf5-505a-5c83-800d-32cdc8dd40d0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$800$ liters" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534381} {:answer/id #uuid "65cd7e27-fab6-576d-9c25-07914a986223" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$2\\,000$ liters" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534471} {:answer/id #uuid "e9602873-3dbc-505a-b6a6-91eb730e5c49" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$10\\,000$ liters" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534784}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0ebdf27a-4c72-5cb8-aa57-5a63e946d3bc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$600$ liters" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534294} :answer-field/id #uuid "33c6bcac-1b81-5ea4-b234-754b353a54a7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534862}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "d4c5ef62-20c9-5f14-af52-91dedd4c1fc7" :question/math-academy-id "q-115676" :question/problem "A tank is filling with soda. The table below gives the rate $R(t)$, in liters per minute, at which the tank is filling at certain moments since it started.\n\n| $t\\,(\\text{minutes})$ | $0$ | $3$ | $6$ | $9$ | $12$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $R(t)\\,(\\text{liters/minute})$ | $30$ | $40$ | $60$ | $70$ | $90$ |\n\nGiven that the tank is empty when $t=0,$ use a left Riemann sum for $R(t)$ over the interval $[0,12]$ with four subintervals to approximate the total volume of soda in the tank after $12$ minutes." :question/worked-solution "First, let's compute the step size $Δt,$ which is just the width of each rectangle. In this case, the endpoints of our interval are $a=0$ and $b=12,$ and there are $n=4$ rectangles, so the step size is\n\n$$\nΔt=\\frac{b-a}{n}=\\frac{12-0}{4}=3.\n$$\n\nThe area $A$ can be approximated using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ≈(\\overbrace{R({t}_{0})+R({t}_{1})+…+R({t}_{n-1})}^{\\text{heights}})\\cdot \\,\\,\\underbrace{Δt}_{\\text{ }\\text{width}} \\\\  & =(R(0)+R(3)+R(6)+R(9))\\cdot Δt.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we now set up a table with the values of $R(t)$ at each point:\n\n| $t$ | $0$ | $3$ | $6$ | $9$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $R(t)$ | $30$ | $40$ | $60$ | $70$ |\n\nNow, we obtain the following approximation for the area:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ≈(30+40+60+70)\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =200\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =600\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nFinally, in the given context, the area under the curve represents the total volume in the tank. So, the volume is $600$ liters."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186535083 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068797 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "26f44f24-4cb6-5d0d-b973-8820171cfff1" :knowledge-point/title "Solving for an Unknown Given the Value of a Left Riemann Sum With Regular Step Size"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0960c82b-6c1b-5b1f-8bf9-41ec598d133a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534278} {:answer/id #uuid "45fc19c2-908b-50ae-ba05-376e1cbaa786" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534398} {:answer/id #uuid "52b56205-1df3-51bc-8170-ccb928677b3b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534429} {:answer/id #uuid "6f8030cb-2dd5-50f4-a3eb-3a5316c29433" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534499} {:answer/id #uuid "9681dbd2-1094-52d5-b2bd-83e405cca6a9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534596}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "52b56205-1df3-51bc-8170-ccb928677b3b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534429} :answer-field/id #uuid "d71bc7f8-343e-57a7-96e4-46b5c22b0949" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534952}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f3cd5193-df6c-5672-95df-52f56264b423" :question/math-academy-id "q-115688" :question/problem "The table below gives the values of a continuous function $f(x)$ over the closed interval $[-3,3]$ with a regular step size. If the left Riemann sum approximation of the area under the curve $y=f(x)$ with $3$ subintervals defined by the given points has a value of $4$, then what must be the value of $k?$\n\n| $x$ | $-3$ | $-1$ | $1$ | $3$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $1$ | $k$ | $4$ | $5$ |" :question/worked-solution "The area $A$ can be approximated using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ≈(\\overbrace{f({x}_{0})+f({x}_{1})+…+f({x}_{n-1})}^{\\text{heights}})\\cdot \\,\\,\\underbrace{Δx}_{\\text{ }\\text{width}} \\\\  & =(f(-3)+f(-1)+f(1))\\cdot Δx.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we now set up a table with the values of $f(x)$ at each point:\n\n| $x$ | $-3$ | $-1$ | $1$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $1$ | $k$ | $4$ |\n\nSo, we obtain the following approximation to the area:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ≈(1+k+4)\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =(5+k)\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =10+2k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since we are given that $A$ must be $4$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}10+2k & =4 \\\\ 2k & =-6 \\\\ k & =-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186535090 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068797 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "26f44f24-4cb6-5d0d-b973-8820171cfff1" :knowledge-point/title "Solving for an Unknown Given the Value of a Left Riemann Sum With Regular Step Size"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "02a2b37e-f7fd-5fb0-af6d-9e771b691d02" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534269} {:answer/id #uuid "5b64cde6-94d5-505a-acf7-2a0725fed336" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534448} {:answer/id #uuid "732c7367-4e22-5a0a-8514-ecf1b997bfbc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534510} {:answer/id #uuid "74ee3ad3-6f87-5264-ad1c-f19f7f542b25" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534518} {:answer/id #uuid "959c80c5-314d-5889-8b74-145a524b94ae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534593}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5b64cde6-94d5-505a-acf7-2a0725fed336" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534448} :answer-field/id #uuid "63889206-4140-5315-b206-7bddd19df74c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186534890}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "fd327109-8d69-58f0-a86a-7b3b70080707" :question/math-academy-id "q-86296" :question/problem "John is selling juice at a market stall. The table below gives the rate $r(t)$ of juice sold over a period of $6$ hours, from $1\\,\\text{p.m.}$ to $7\\,\\text{p.m.}$\n\n| $t\\,(\\text{hours})$ | $1$ | $3$ | $5$ | $7$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $r(t)\\,(\\text{liters/hour})$ | $k$ | $2$ | $3$ | $4$ |\n\nIf the left Riemann sum approximation of the total number of liters John sold during the given period has a value of $16$ liters, what is the value of $k?$" :question/worked-solution "The area $A$ under the curve $y=r(t)$ can be approximated using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ≈(\\overbrace{r({t}_{0})+r({t}_{1})+…+r({t}_{n-1})}^{\\text{heights}})\\cdot \\,\\,\\underbrace{Δt}_{\\text{ }\\text{width}} \\\\  & =(r(1)+r(3)+r(5))\\cdot Δt.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we now set up a table with the values of $r(t)$ at each point:\n\n| $t$ | $1$ | $3$ | $5$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $r(t)$ | $k$ | $2$ | $3$ |\n\nSo, we obtain the following approximation to the area:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ≈(k+2+3)\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =2(k+5) \\\\  & =2k+10\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the given context, the area under the curve represents the total number of liters sold by John. Since we are given that the left Riemann sum approximation of the total number of liters sold is $16,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2k+10 & =16 \\\\ 2k & =6 \\\\ k & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186535929 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105721 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5f0efa50-9154-5cf3-bf01-e1b6eead489d" :knowledge-point/title "Using the Right Riemann Sum With Regular Step Size"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "878774eb-c7dd-5a87-917e-76cf95c77c8a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.25" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535651} {:answer/id #uuid "afa5e8c5-9f06-528f-8ca9-ee3e619e89fb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.75" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535704} {:answer/id #uuid "d1153c2a-51a4-5211-961e-435b0d35c3ea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.75" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535752} {:answer/id #uuid "d228caf4-6c0e-5539-84ab-bf4720527798" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535757} {:answer/id #uuid "d6ba9520-4205-5f57-8874-c6ed0a363e74" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535763}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "878774eb-c7dd-5a87-917e-76cf95c77c8a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.25" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535651} :answer-field/id #uuid "b2488740-ed19-518a-aec2-7d83e9f3a9a5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535898}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "0fbb26d4-3faf-5389-98e4-54735a0cfc0b" :question/math-academy-id "q-115697" :question/problem "Estimate the area under the curve $y=-{x}^{2}+2x$ over the interval $[0,2]$ using a right Riemann sum with step size $Δx=0.5.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's sketch our situation.\n\n![](images/0b/0b9dd2388a7b60866eb15d336d51d181853fe5ac0e52dcd3e02f53fdd4848532.png)\n\nThe area $A$ under the graph can be approximated using the formula\n\n$$\nA≈(\\overbrace{f(0.5)+f(1)+f(1.5)+f(2)}^{\\text{heights}})\\cdot \\underbrace{Δx}_{\\text{width}}.\n$$\n\nSo, we now set up a table with the values of $f(x)$ at each point:\n\n| $x$ | $0.5$ | $1$ | $1.5$ | $2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $0.75$ | $1$ | $0.75$ | $0$ |\n\nFinally, we obtain the following approximation for our area:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ≈(0.75+1+0.75+0)\\cdot 0.5 \\\\  & =2.5\\cdot 0.5 \\\\  & =1.25\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186535930 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105815 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ad9b9344-1711-5ff4-b489-f6ef2a0bd2eb" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether a Right Riemann Sum is an Overestimate or Underestimate"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "17e7ee46-c0a0-5388-b5c3-f743c9eeeff3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$5.25,$ overestimate" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535472} {:answer/id #uuid "8491a6b6-27ba-5890-9894-2dbd35131e9e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$5.25,$ underestimate" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535644} {:answer/id #uuid "bae36594-f50c-5335-98b1-7ed2a0ea25e7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$5.50,$ underestimate" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535721} {:answer/id #uuid "e21421ad-d404-568a-b6ff-34207debc096" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$4.25,$ overestimate" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535783} {:answer/id #uuid "ee374620-40ba-583d-8548-625378a12bdb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$4.25,$ underestimate" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535802}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ee374620-40ba-583d-8548-625378a12bdb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$4.25,$ underestimate" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535802} :answer-field/id #uuid "ef28899d-b306-5e8e-a4d3-bef109a6e779" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535918}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "10bc8e6f-4c2c-5a63-aa64-53c9dbd12a9a" :question/math-academy-id "q-20794" :question/problem "Estimate the area under the curve $y=4-{x}^{2}$ over the interval $[0,2]$ using a right Riemann sum with step size $Δx=0.5.$ Is this an overestimate or an underestimate?" :question/worked-solution "Let's sketch our situation.\n\n![](images/5c/5c52bbe248df0e5a595298d978276023bcb681daa904f37e2818013687aab52e.png)\n\nThe area $A$ under the graph can be approximated using the formula\n\n$$\nA≈(\\overbrace{f(0.5)+f(1)+f(1.5)+f(2)}^{\\text{heights}})\\cdot \\underbrace{Δx}_{\\text{width}}.\n$$\n\nSo, we now set up a table with the values of $f(x)$ at each point:\n\n| $x$ | $0.5$ | $1$ | $1.5$ | $2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $3.75$ | $3$ | $1.75$ | $0$ |\n\nFinally, we obtain the following approximation for our area:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ≈(3.75+3+1.75+0)\\cdot 0.5 \\\\  & =8.50\\cdot 0.5 \\\\  & =4.25\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBecause the function is *strictly decreasing* over the interval $[0,2],$ the rectangles reach *below* the function, which means our approximation is an *underestimate* of the correct area."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186535934 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105721 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5f0efa50-9154-5cf3-bf01-e1b6eead489d" :knowledge-point/title "Using the Right Riemann Sum With Regular Step Size"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b60d1c76-997e-5fea-b00b-ccb5d53f5fa6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "620" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535714}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b60d1c76-997e-5fea-b00b-ccb5d53f5fa6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "620" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535714} :answer-field/id #uuid "14d74c82-2922-5d9b-8164-dac9534530d8" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535838}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "18df9f2e-656d-5486-9ae1-27119d2f448d" :question/math-academy-id "q-316517" :question/problem "The following table shows the water flow rate, $R(t),$ through a pipe (in liters per minute) over a period of $40$ minutes.\n\n| $t\\,\\text{(minutes)}$ | $0$ | $10$ | $20$ | $30$ | $40$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $R\\,\\text{(liters/minute)}$ | $12$ | $15$ | $17$ | $16$ | $14$ |\n\nUse the right Riemann sum for $R(t)$ over the interval $[0,40]$ with four subintervals to approximate the total volume of water pumped.\n\nThe total volume is \n{{field-1}} liters." :question/worked-solution "First, let's compute the step size $Δt,$ which is just the width of each rectangle. In this case, the endpoints of our interval are $a=0$ and $b=40,$ and there are $n=4$ rectangles, so the step size is\n\n$$\nΔt=\\frac{b-a}{n}=\\frac{40-0}{4}=10.\n$$\n\nThe area $A$ under the graph can be approximated using the formula\n\n$$\nA≈(\\overbrace{R(10)+R(20)+R(30)+R(40)}^{\\text{heights}})\\cdot \\underbrace{Δt}_{\\text{width}}.\n$$\n\nSo, we now set up a table with the values of $R(t)$ at each point:\n\n| $t$ | $10$ | $20$ | $30$ | $40$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $R(t)$ | $15$ | $17$ | $16$ | $14$ |\n\nNow, we obtain the following approximation for the area:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ≈(15+17+16+14)\\cdot 10 \\\\  & =62\\cdot 10 \\\\  & =620\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, in the given context, the area under the curve represents the total volume of water pumped over a period of $40$ minutes. So, the total volume is $620$ liters."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186535941 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105814 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "01fe05cd-8acc-5676-9328-7a26362a77d0" :knowledge-point/title "Solving for an Unknown Given the Value of a Right Riemann Sum With Regular Step Size"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0dcef793-e9bd-548e-8171-f1c2694d465b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535461}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0dcef793-e9bd-548e-8171-f1c2694d465b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535461} :answer-field/id #uuid "d3ccd5f4-a37a-56a2-9400-43d3c5f9830f" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535911}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "295aa24d-cc18-5ddf-b640-8d50f96f1e1a" :question/math-academy-id "q-346446" :question/problem "The following table shows the cooling rate of a walk-in freezer over a period of $10$ hours, starting at $9\\,\\text{a.m}.$\n\n| $t\\,(\\text{hours})$ | $9$ | $11$ | $13$ | $15$ | $17$ | $19$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $R(t)\\,(\\text{kilojoules/hour})$ | $5$ | $4$ | $3$ | $2$ | $4$ | $k$ |\n\n If the right Riemann sum approximation of the total energy removed from $9\\,\\text{a.m}$ to $7\\,\\text{p.m.}$ has a value of $40$ kilojoules, then what is the value of $k?$ \n\n$k=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The area $A$ under the curve (rate vs. time) can be approximated using the formula\n\n$$\nA≈(\\overbrace{R(11)+R(13)+R(15)+R(17)+R(19)}^{\\text{heights}})\\cdot \\underbrace{Δt}_{\\text{width}}.\n$$\n\nSo, we now set up a table with the values of the rate at each point:\n\n| $t$ | $11$ | $13$ | $15$ | $17$ | $19$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $R(t)$ | $4$ | $3$ | $2$ | $4$ | $k$ |\n\nNow, we obtain the following approximation for the area:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ≈(4+3+2+4+k)\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =2(k+13) \\\\  & =2k+26\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, in the given context, the area under the curve represents the total energy removed from $9\\,\\text{a.m}$ to $7\\,\\text{p.m}.$ Since we are given that the right Riemann sum approximation of the total energy removed is $40$ kilojoules, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2k+26 & =40 \\\\ 2k & =40-26 \\\\ k & =\\frac{14}{2} \\\\ k & =7.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186535943 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105721 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5f0efa50-9154-5cf3-bf01-e1b6eead489d" :knowledge-point/title "Using the Right Riemann Sum With Regular Step Size"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7196d172-1722-59c9-8d40-bba7f5842eaf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "32" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535607}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7196d172-1722-59c9-8d40-bba7f5842eaf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "32" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535607} :answer-field/id #uuid "53ef4dc0-4c26-5272-80ba-0abca0bed270" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535861}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "2e5afc97-cdbf-5609-994c-66b16cc595b4" :question/math-academy-id "q-346101" :question/problem "The following table shows the fuel consumption rate, $F(t),$ of a generator (in liters per hour) over a period of $8$ hours.\n\n| $t\\,\\text{(hours)}$ | $0$ | $2$ | $4$ | $6$ | $8$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $F\\,\\text{(liters/hour)}$ | $4$ | $3$ | $5$ | $2$ | $6$ |\n\nUse the right Riemann sum for $F(t)$ over the interval $[0,8]$ with four subintervals to approximate the total amount of fuel used.\n\nThe total amount of fuel is \n{{field-1}} liters." :question/worked-solution "First, let's compute the step size $Δt,$ which is just the width of each rectangle. In this case, the endpoints of our interval are $a=0$ and $b=8,$ and there are $n=4$ rectangles, so the step size is\n\n$$\nΔt=\\frac{b-a}{n}=\\frac{8-0}{4}=2.\n$$\n\nThe area $A$ under the graph can be approximated using the formula\n\n$$\nA≈(\\overbrace{F(2)+F(4)+F(6)+F(8)}^{\\text{heights}})\\cdot \\underbrace{Δt}_{\\text{width}}.\n$$\n\nSo, we now set up a table with the values of $F(t)$ at each point:\n\n| $t$ | $2$ | $4$ | $6$ | $8$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $F(t)$ | $3$ | $5$ | $2$ | $6$ |\n\nNow, we obtain the following approximation for the area:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ≈(3+5+2+6)\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =16\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =32\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, in the given context, the area under the curve represents the total amount of fuel used over a period of $8$ hours. So, the total amount of fuel is $32$ liters."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186535950 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229132 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4eca32dd-1563-55c6-b594-08588efd601b" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Inhomogeneous System Using a Given Matrix Exponential"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d4f64bf3-528b-508d-afe7-7fb41040696f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\cos(t)-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535760}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d4f64bf3-528b-508d-afe7-7fb41040696f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\cos(t)-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535760} :answer-field/id #uuid "c0cddfae-ecf5-50fd-9bfc-04c2102bb55f" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535905}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "5cb555db-6a59-509d-947e-27c4f1a2a7e6" :question/math-academy-id "q-335062" :question/problem "$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=y(t)+1 \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=-x(t),\\end{aligned}\\,[\\begin{aligned}x(0) \\\\ y(0)\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nFind the solution to the initial value problem above.\n\n*Hint: ${e}^{At}=[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t) & \\operatorname{sin}⁡(t) \\\\ -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t) & \\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)\\end{aligned}],$ where $A=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}].$*\n\n<table align=\"l\" cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"30px\" rowspan=\"2\">$[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}]=\\,\\,$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"40px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"120px\">$2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-1}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>" :question/worked-solution "Writing our system in matrix form, we get\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}]x(t)+g(t),\n$$\n\nwhere $x(t)=[\\begin{aligned}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{aligned}]$ and $g(t)=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].$ So, the matrix of the system is $A=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}].$\n\nGiven the system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)+g(t),$ where $A$ is a constant square matrix, the solution of the initial value problem can be written as the sum of the homogeneous part and a particular solution:\n\n$$\nx(t)=\\underbrace{{e}^{A(t-{t}_{0})}\\,x({t}_{0})}_{\\text{homogeneous part}}+\\underbrace{{\\int }_{{t}_{0}}^{t}{e}^{A(t-s)}g(s)\\,\\text{d}s}_{\\text{particular solution}},\n$$\n\nwhere $x({t}_{0})$ is the initial condition vector. \n\nLet's evaluate each part separately:\n\n- First, we find the homogeneous part. We are given that \n\n$$\n{e}^{At}=[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t) & \\operatorname{sin}⁡(t) \\\\ -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t) & \\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nSince the initial conditions are given at $t={t}_{0}=0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{A(t-{t}_{0})} & ={e}^{At}\\,x(0) \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t) & \\operatorname{sin}⁡(t) \\\\ -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t) & \\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t) \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Next, we find the particular solution. Notice that \n\n$$\n{e}^{A(t-s)}=[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t-s) & \\operatorname{sin}⁡(t-s) \\\\ -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t-s) & \\operatorname{cos}⁡(t-s)\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{A(t-s)}g(s) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t-s) & \\operatorname{sin}⁡(t-s) \\\\ -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t-s) & \\operatorname{cos}⁡(t-s)\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t-s) \\\\ -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t-s)\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{t}{e}^{A(t-s)}g(s)\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{t}[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t-s) \\\\ -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t-s)\\end{aligned}]\\text{d}s \\\\  & ={[\\begin{aligned}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t-s) \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(t-s)\\end{aligned}]}_{s=0}^{s=t} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t) \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)-1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t) \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t) \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)-1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t) \\\\ 2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)-1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186535960 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105721 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5f0efa50-9154-5cf3-bf01-e1b6eead489d" :knowledge-point/title "Using the Right Riemann Sum With Regular Step Size"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "01050c59-9553-589f-9c48-f5d7d72b8958" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$2\\,220$ flour bags" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535437} {:answer/id #uuid "40d7d78b-066c-592b-9585-d48edeb29892" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$1\\,920$ flour bags" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535533} {:answer/id #uuid "80f4d759-7dd2-58ba-b661-c508f8284e2a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$2\\,400$ flour bags" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535639} {:answer/id #uuid "d17f65ac-d738-5edc-866a-4144aae04c51" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$2\\,100$ flour bags" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535755} {:answer/id #uuid "f66356a1-a60a-5a2c-9291-629272352032" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$1\\,600$ flour bags" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535811}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "80f4d759-7dd2-58ba-b661-c508f8284e2a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$2\\,400$ flour bags" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535639} :answer-field/id #uuid "60b2584c-7157-5847-8956-d79d5f653369" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535865}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "7984804e-ee09-5ab8-9ea3-cfb9bf84394d" :question/math-academy-id "q-115745" :question/problem "The following table shows the rate $r(t)$, in flour bags per minute, at which a machine makes flour over a period of $75$ minutes.\n\n| $t\\,\\text{(minutes)}$ | $0$ | $15$ | $30$ | $45$ | $60$ | $75$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $r\\,\\text{(flour bags/minute)}$ | $30$ | $25$ | $35$ | $40$ | $35$ | $25$ |\n\nUse the right Riemann sum for $r(t)$ over the interval $[0,75]$, with five subintervals, to approximate the number of flour bags made by the machine in $75$ minutes." :question/worked-solution "First, let's compute the step size $Δt,$ which is just the width of each rectangle. In this case, the endpoints of our interval are $a=0$ and $b=75,$ and there are $n=5$ rectangles, so the step size is\n\n$$\nΔt=\\frac{b-a}{n}=\\frac{75-0}{5}=15.\n$$\n\nThe area $A$ under the graph can be approximated using the formula\n\n$$\nA≈(\\overbrace{r(15)+r(30)+r(45)+r(60)+r(75)}^{\\text{heights}})\\cdot \\underbrace{Δt}_{\\text{width}}.\n$$\n\nSo, we now set up a table with the values of $r(t)$ at each point:\n\n| $t$ | $15$ | $30$ | $45$ | $60$ | $75$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $r(t)$ | $25$ | $35$ | $40$ | $35$ | $25$ |\n\nNow, we obtain the following approximation for the area:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ≈(25+35+40+35+25)\\cdot 15 \\\\  & =160\\cdot 15 \\\\  & =2\\,400\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nFinally, in the given context, the area under the curve represents the total number of flour bags made by the machine in $75$ minutes.  So, the number of flour bags is approximately $2\\,400.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186535963 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105814 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "01fe05cd-8acc-5676-9328-7a26362a77d0" :knowledge-point/title "Solving for an Unknown Given the Value of a Right Riemann Sum With Regular Step Size"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0d8c4dd0-b4fb-5f1d-970c-93e234d6c211" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "15" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535460} {:answer/id #uuid "11aa1a77-5ad4-5bbc-8d24-035cfb0fffc1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "22" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535465} {:answer/id #uuid "515a0644-5d83-50b2-b738-12e33dae1bc6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "20" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535560} {:answer/id #uuid "a0746224-bc7e-5b08-b957-2482f2935120" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "23" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535685} {:answer/id #uuid "d73cdc93-d689-5be0-b050-c70c2fe6ce12" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "25" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535766}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0d8c4dd0-b4fb-5f1d-970c-93e234d6c211" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "15" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535460} :answer-field/id #uuid "7f5bea90-ec71-5ac4-a16e-8e74f85dff8c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535877}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "86b94149-e5f8-5f16-b1b9-921b18ee69eb" :question/math-academy-id "q-86344" :question/problem "The following table gives the speed of a minivan measured during a $4$-second interval.\n\n| $t\\,(\\text{seconds})$ | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ | $4$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $v(t)\\,(\\text{meters/second})$ | $17$ | $18$ | $k$ | $25$ | $27$ |\n\n If the right Riemann sum approximation of the total distance that the minivan traveled during the $4$-second interval has a value of $85$ meters, what is the value of $k?$\n\n*Hint: The distance traveled is given by the area under the speed-time graph.*" :question/worked-solution "The area $A$ under the curve $y=v(t)$ can be approximated using the formula\n\n$$\nA≈(\\overbrace{v(1)+v(2)+v(3)+v(4)}^{\\text{heights}})\\cdot \\underbrace{Δt}_{\\text{width}}.\n$$\n\nSo, we now set up a table with the values of $v(t)$ at each point:\n\n| $t$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ | $4$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $v(t)$ | $18$ | $k$ | $25$ | $27$ |\n\nNow, we obtain the following approximation for the area:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ≈(18+k+25+27)\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =70+k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, in the given context, the area under the curve represents the total distance that the minivan traveled. Since we are given that the right Riemann sum approximation of the total distance is $85$ meters,  we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}70+k & =85 \\\\ k & =15.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186535967 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105814 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "01fe05cd-8acc-5676-9328-7a26362a77d0" :knowledge-point/title "Solving for an Unknown Given the Value of a Right Riemann Sum With Regular Step Size"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3a4bda3f-13a2-588d-8d36-9540552bfd8c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535523}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3a4bda3f-13a2-588d-8d36-9540552bfd8c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535523} :answer-field/id #uuid "438cb7c6-cefd-5543-b39d-e0a02ae170e4" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535855}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "8de05636-df00-52b0-a8f6-5c5d3db73b2e" :question/math-academy-id "q-346443" :question/problem "The following table shows the water drainage rate of a reservoir over a period of $8$ hours, starting at $2\\,\\text{p.m}.$\n\n| $t\\,(\\text{hours})$ | $2$ | $4$ | $6$ | $8$ | $10$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $R(t)\\,(\\text{gallons/hour})$ | $2$ | $k$ | $5$ | $4$ | $3$ |\n\nIf the right Riemann sum approximation of the total water drained from $2\\,\\text{p.m}$ to $10\\,\\text{p.m.}$ has a value of $30$ gallons, then what is the value of $k?$ \n\n$k=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The area $A$ under the curve (rate vs. time) can be approximated using the formula\n\n$$\nA≈(\\overbrace{R(4)+R(6)+R(8)+R(10)}^{\\text{heights}})\\cdot \\underbrace{Δt}_{\\text{width}}.\n$$\n\nSo, we now set up a table with the values of the rate at each point:\n\n| $t$ | $4$ | $6$ | $8$ | $10$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $R(t)$ | $k$ | $5$ | $4$ | $3$ |\n\nNow, we obtain the following approximation for the area:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ≈(k+5+4+3)\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =2(k+12) \\\\  & =2k+24\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, in the given context, the area under the curve represents the total water drained from $2\\,\\text{p.m}$ to $10\\,\\text{p.m}.$ Since we are given that the right Riemann sum approximation of the total drainage is $30$ gallons, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2k+24 & =30 \\\\ 2k & =30-24 \\\\ k & =\\frac{6}{2} \\\\ k & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186535975 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105815 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ad9b9344-1711-5ff4-b489-f6ef2a0bd2eb" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether a Right Riemann Sum is an Overestimate or Underestimate"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "002a6a31-b58c-5580-9148-3001495a4d7a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$7,$ overestimate" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535435} {:answer/id #uuid "42e96206-5932-5b4b-b71e-f61b37f74d76" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$8,$ overestimate" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535539} {:answer/id #uuid "6ebf40b5-4be5-5ebe-9a26-6c0c7cbdff35" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$6.5,$ overestimate" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535604} {:answer/id #uuid "7d68cf48-ca72-5f17-9243-c2b8260ae54c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$7,$ underestimate" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535629} {:answer/id #uuid "e5aa91ea-a144-5bc9-bee2-f4c14cf6d192" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$6.5,$ underestimate" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535787}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "002a6a31-b58c-5580-9148-3001495a4d7a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$7,$ overestimate" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535435} :answer-field/id #uuid "58ee3ca7-e743-5c98-a18c-ba847bc17d29" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535864}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "9e633987-af32-5b5c-bd64-b66c42ee580f" :question/math-academy-id "q-74337" :question/problem "Estimate the area under the curve $y=2x+3$ over the interval $[-1,1]$ using a right Riemann sum with step size $Δx=0.5.$ Is this an overestimate or an underestimate?" :question/worked-solution "Let's sketch our situation.\n\n![](images/be/be5f6207531c18acecdb6a7ba3890e23913de4a78546db620f2986dd0467fa6d.png)\n\nThe area $A$ under the graph can be approximated using the formula\n\n$$\nA≈(\\overbrace{f(-0.5)+f(0)+f(0.5)+f(1)}^{\\text{heights}})\\cdot \\underbrace{Δx}_{\\text{width}}.\n$$\n\nSo, we now set up a table with the values of $f(x)$ at each point:\n\n| $x$ | $-0.5$ | $0$ | $0.5$ | $1$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $2$ | $3$ | $4$ | $5$ |\n\nFinally, we obtain the following approximation for our area:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ≈(2+3+4+5)\\cdot 0.5 \\\\  & =14\\cdot 0.5 \\\\  & =7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBecause the function is *strictly increasing* over the interval $[-1,1],$ the rectangles reach *above* the function, which means our approximation is an *overestimate* of the correct area."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186535995 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105814 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "01fe05cd-8acc-5676-9328-7a26362a77d0" :knowledge-point/title "Solving for an Unknown Given the Value of a Right Riemann Sum With Regular Step Size"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "07357d7a-2f83-5eb7-92b8-b510a24224cf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535449} {:answer/id #uuid "1894293b-2266-5249-81b6-4ceec74dc5e5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "15" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535473} {:answer/id #uuid "34734b2d-4a62-530e-a6bd-694b62c062aa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535513} {:answer/id #uuid "41113075-3263-5183-b6a6-417bd1862926" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535534} {:answer/id #uuid "b72a414b-c009-5184-b3b0-8ca0bca96051" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535716}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "07357d7a-2f83-5eb7-92b8-b510a24224cf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535449} :answer-field/id #uuid "b3c699ed-502d-50f7-85eb-b0f04825f3f6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535899}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d36beb24-ba8a-5d0f-80e1-d03ab3463ddd" :question/math-academy-id "q-49092" :question/problem "The table below gives the values of a continuous function $f(x)$ on the closed interval $[0,15]$ with a regular step size. If the right Riemann sum approximation to the area under the curve $y=f(x)$ with $3$ subintervals defined by the given points has a value of $60$, then what must be the value of $k?$\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $5$ | $10$ | $15$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $2$ | $3$ | $4$ | $k$ |" :question/worked-solution "The area $A$ under the graph can be approximated using the formula\n\n$$\nA≈(\\overbrace{f(5)+f(10)+f(15)}^{\\text{heights}})\\cdot \\underbrace{Δx}_{\\text{width}}.\n$$\n\nSo, we now set up a table with the values of $f(x)$ at each point:\n\n| $x$ | $5$ | $10$ | $15$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $3$ | $4$ | $k$ |\n\nNow, we obtain the following approximation for the area:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ≈(3+4+k)\\cdot 5 \\\\  & =(7+k)\\cdot 5 \\\\  & =5k+35\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since we are given that $A$ must be  $60,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5k+35 & =60 \\\\ 5k & =60-35 \\\\ k & =\\frac{25}{5} \\\\ k & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186536005 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105814 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "01fe05cd-8acc-5676-9328-7a26362a77d0" :knowledge-point/title "Solving for an Unknown Given the Value of a Right Riemann Sum With Regular Step Size"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "240701c7-693d-5227-90b8-2a6284ad88fb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "14" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535491} {:answer/id #uuid "57bfefc1-dc04-51d9-be61-49334d95d8ad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "20" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535575} {:answer/id #uuid "8cec8f0e-46be-5259-81e8-bcdb5a4a53a8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535659} {:answer/id #uuid "d8fcc852-79de-51f7-9ec6-886cb9fb714d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "18" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535767} {:answer/id #uuid "ea2ae6d8-8214-5546-b570-caa5929f2b72" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "22" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535797}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "57bfefc1-dc04-51d9-be61-49334d95d8ad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "20" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535575} :answer-field/id #uuid "7023ddcf-d3c1-52ff-bd11-0bc5ea2c28b6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186535870}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "fbb4c94f-2803-5b7e-b57a-776b92b59056" :question/math-academy-id "q-86372" :question/problem "The table below gives some values of a continuous function $f(t)$ on the closed interval $[10,40].$ The right Riemann sum approximation to the area under the curve $y=f(t)$ with $3$ equal subintervals has a value of $480.$ What must be the value of $k?$\n\n| $t$ | $10$ | $20$ | $30$ | $40$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(t)$ | $18$ | $12$ | $k$ | $16$ |" :question/worked-solution "The area $A$ can be approximated using the formula\n\n$$\nA≈(\\overbrace{f(20)+f(30)+f(40)}^{\\text{heights}})\\cdot \\underbrace{Δt}_{\\text{width}}.\n$$\n\nSo, we now set up a table with the values of $f(t)$ at each point:\n\n| $t$ | $20$ | $30$ | $40$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(t)$ | $12$ | $k$ | $16$ |\n\nSo, we obtain the following approximation to the area:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ≈(12+k+16)\\cdot 10 \\\\  & =(28+k)\\cdot 10\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince we are given that $A$ must be $480$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(28+k)\\cdot 10 & =480 \\\\ 28+k & =48 \\\\ k & =20.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186536897 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064258 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0649943a-643f-592f-8815-45c871f7e7dd" :knowledge-point/title "Splitting a Rational Expression With Three Terms in the Numerator"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2385595f-7f7d-5bcb-b6db-b0de1f32a65b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$a=2,$ $b=-2$ and $c=-1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536334} {:answer/id #uuid "5bd91c40-af18-5c9d-b794-20c583df8cb8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$a=\\frac{1}{5},$ $b=2$ and $c=\\frac{2}{5}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536444} {:answer/id #uuid "86d1e6a6-32cc-5370-9d0c-04db2526ab57" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$a=1,$ $b=-2$ and $c=2$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536503} {:answer/id #uuid "bd7d356d-5fef-5042-be30-4689b4b9777a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$a=1,$ $b=\\frac{1}{5}$ and $c=-\\frac{2}{5}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536583} {:answer/id #uuid "fc3e3b6b-5c5d-5998-9365-602913fed818" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$a=1,$ $b=-2$ and $c=-\\frac{2}{5}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536687}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fc3e3b6b-5c5d-5998-9365-602913fed818" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$a=1,$ $b=-2$ and $c=-\\frac{2}{5}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536687} :answer-field/id #uuid "9ac144df-2131-57c9-bfbb-43bd44e355e9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536762}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f3e4db00-9ed0-5e91-8213-2ca14aefffbb" :question/math-academy-id "q-103625" :question/problem "If $\\frac{5x-10y-2z}{5}$ is written in the form $ax+by+cz,$ what are the values of  $a,$ $b$ and $c?$" :question/worked-solution "Splitting the fraction into separate terms and then simplifying the fractions in the individual terms, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5x-10y-2z}{5} & = \\\\ \\frac{5x}{5}-\\frac{10y}{5}-\\frac{2z}{5} & = \\\\ \\frac{5}{5}\\cdot x-\\frac{10}{5}\\cdot y-\\frac{2}{5}\\cdot z & = \\\\ 1\\cdot x-2\\cdot y-\\frac{2}{5}\\cdot z & = \\\\ x-2y-\\frac{2z}{5} & .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting the above in the form $ax+by+cz,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\underbrace{(1)}_{a}x+\\underbrace{(-2)}_{b}y+\\underbrace{(-\\frac{2}{5})}_{c}z.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,  $a=1,$ $b=-2$ and $c=-\\frac{2}{5}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186537977 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186101018 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f2d19236-d6ea-57da-b2a4-b926fdddb30e" :knowledge-point/title "Maximizing a Revenue Function Given a Price Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1d62f0bd-935b-5b00-a2c0-88c9776e3a4f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "15" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537291} {:answer/id #uuid "5069e6da-e9ae-553f-8f4a-25dc832f7acc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "20" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537397} {:answer/id #uuid "7325bf9e-6b3c-590c-8efe-c6c988414b52" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537456} {:answer/id #uuid "b9910e6d-7274-5ad8-9582-87f427993b41" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537590} {:answer/id #uuid "bfddd2cc-fb5b-506f-910b-2fc1d8be0dba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "17" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537601}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1d62f0bd-935b-5b00-a2c0-88c9776e3a4f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "15" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537291} :answer-field/id #uuid "465ed207-d844-5919-a8df-b87293b0bcf1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537780}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "da64bda4-339a-50c3-828b-c79ac0fe60b0" :question/math-academy-id "q-87576" :question/problem "A daycare center can take care of between $10$ and $20$ children. The fee that parents must pay each month depends on the number of children enrolled in the center. If there are $10$ children enrolled, parents must pay $$40.$ On the other hand, if there are more than $10$ children enrolled, the price drops by $$2$ for each child. All the parents pay the same monthly fee.\n\nHow many children must enroll for the center to maximize its revenue?" :question/worked-solution "First, note that if there are $10+x$ children enrolled, then the monthly fee is $40-2x.$ So, the total revenue is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R(x) & =\\underbrace{(10+x)}_{\\text{number of children enrolled}}\\times \\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\underbrace{(40-2x)}_{\\text{monthly fee}} \\\\  & =(10+x)(40-2x) \\\\  & =400-20x+40x-2{x}^{2} \\\\  & =400+20x-2{x}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the center can only take care between $10$ and $20$ children, the permitted domain for $x$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}10 & \\le x+10\\le 20 \\\\ 0 & \\le x\\le 10.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $x=0$ and $x=10$ are both candidates for the global maxima of $R(x).$\n\nWe need to find the critical points of $R(x)$ for $0<x<10.$ Taking the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n{R}^{′}(x)=20-4x,\n$$\n\nand solving ${R}^{′}(x)=0$ gives the following critical point:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{R}^{′}(x) & =0 \\\\ 20-4x & =0 \\\\ 4x & =20 \\\\ x & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that $x=5$ lies in the interval $0<x<10.$ All that's left now is to confirm that $x=5$ does indeed give a maximum value of $R(x).$ To do this, we compute the second derivative:\n\n$$\n{R}^{″}(x)=-4<0\n$$\n\nSince the second derivative is negative, the point $x=5$ is indeed a local maximum of $R(x).$ And since $R(x)$ is quadratic, we conclude that $x=5$ is a global maximum of $R(x).$ \n\nTherefore, the center must have $10+5=15$ children enrolled to maximize its revenue."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186541124 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229131 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "95223ee8-46d8-58c2-9e3b-017adb2789e8" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Matrix Exponential Formula for IVP Solution of an Inhomogeneous System"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "37a88885-e7e0-5479-b8d2-d85e774bbf74" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "e^{2s}\\cos(2(t-s))" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540615}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "37a88885-e7e0-5479-b8d2-d85e774bbf74" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "e^{2s}\\cos(2(t-s))" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540615} :answer-field/id #uuid "5681283e-4465-523e-8b0e-208f5dbcb496" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540980} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "814c0838-d0d0-5c60-bb5d-d662eb334c3a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3\\sin(2(t-1))+\\cos(2(t-1))" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540745}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "814c0838-d0d0-5c60-bb5d-d662eb334c3a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3\\sin(2(t-1))+\\cos(2(t-1))" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540745} :answer-field/id #uuid "c5f719c4-a5bf-542d-85f1-a0d87e09bdf0" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541029}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "b6b0c13d-ace9-58a0-a5ae-835939f481e0" :question/math-academy-id "q-334926" :question/problem "$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 0\\end{aligned}]x+[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{2t} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,x(1)=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nWrite down the expression that gives the solution of the initial value problem above.\n\n*Hint: ${e}^{At}=[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t) & \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) \\\\ -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) & \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)\\end{aligned}],$ where $A=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 0\\end{aligned}].$*\n\n<table align=\"l\" cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"30px\" rowspan=\"2\">$x(t)=\\,\\,$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"40px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"180px\">$3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2(t-1))+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2(t-1))$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"30px\" rowspan=\"2\">$\\,\\,+{\\int }_{1}^{t}\\,\\,$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"40px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"180px\">{{field-1}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"30px\" rowspan=\"2\">$\\,\\,\\text{d}s$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-2}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">$-{e}^{2s}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2(t-s))$</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>" :question/worked-solution "Given the system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)+g(t),$ where $A$ is a constant square matrix, the solution of the initial value problem can be written as the sum of the homogeneous part and a particular solution:\n\n$$\nx(t)=\\underbrace{{e}^{A(t-{t}_{0})}\\,x({t}_{0})}_{\\text{homogeneous part}}+\\underbrace{{\\int }_{{t}_{0}}^{t}{e}^{A(t-s)}g(s)\\,\\text{d}s}_{\\text{particular solution}},\n$$\n\nwhere $x({t}_{0})$ is the initial condition vector. \n\nLet's evaluate each part separately:\n\n- First, we find the homogeneous part. We are given that ${e}^{At}=[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t) & \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) \\\\ -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) & \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)\\end{aligned}].$ So,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{A(t-1)}\\,x(1) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2(t-1)) & \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2(t-1)) \\\\ -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2(t-1)) & \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2(t-1))\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2(t-1))+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2(t-1)) \\\\ -3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2(t-1))+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2(t-1))\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Next, we find the particular solution. Notice that \n\n$$\n{e}^{A(t-s)}=[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2(t-s)) & \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2(t-s)) \\\\ -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2(t-s)) & \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2(t-s))\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{A(t-s)}g(s) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2(t-s)) & \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2(t-s)) \\\\ -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2(t-s)) & \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2(t-s))\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{e}^{2s} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{2s}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2(t-s)) \\\\ -{e}^{2s}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2(t-s))\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & =[\\begin{aligned}3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2(t-1))+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2(t-1)) \\\\ -3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2(t-1))+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2(t-1))\\end{aligned}]+{\\int }_{1}^{t}[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{2s}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2(t-s)) \\\\ -{e}^{2s}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2(t-s))\\end{aligned}]\\text{d}s.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186541149 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229131 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "95223ee8-46d8-58c2-9e3b-017adb2789e8" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Matrix Exponential Formula for IVP Solution of an Inhomogeneous System"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9ca048f2-d590-539c-b053-4530bc08dac8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "e^s\\cos(t-s)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540789}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9ca048f2-d590-539c-b053-4530bc08dac8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "e^s\\cos(t-s)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540789} :answer-field/id #uuid "3eb19d6e-883e-50f6-b7d5-7815f100a541" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540969} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "91956acd-af3c-5284-88d0-d96e76f54ec9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2\\sin(t-1)+3\\cos(t-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540772}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "91956acd-af3c-5284-88d0-d96e76f54ec9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2\\sin(t-1)+3\\cos(t-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540772} :answer-field/id #uuid "4a78e8e2-e93a-5656-bb35-a08702392163" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186540977}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "ed2f4962-bfe0-5321-8b55-e0e1d5307252" :question/math-academy-id "q-334928" :question/problem "$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}]x+[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{t} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\\,x(1)=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nWrite down the expression that gives the solution of the initial value problem above.\n\n*Hint: ${e}^{At}=[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t) & \\operatorname{sin}⁡(t) \\\\ -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t) & \\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)\\end{aligned}],$ where $A=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}].$*\n\n<table align=\"l\" cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"30px\" rowspan=\"2\">$x(t)=\\,\\,$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"40px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"190px\">$2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t-1)+3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t-1)$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"30px\" rowspan=\"2\">$\\,\\,+{\\int }_{1}^{t}\\,\\,$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"40px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"100px\">{{field-1}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"30px\" rowspan=\"2\">$\\,\\,\\text{d}s$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-2}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">$-{e}^{s}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t-s)$</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>" :question/worked-solution "Given the system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)+g(t),$ where $A$ is a constant square matrix, the solution of the initial value problem can be written as the sum of the homogeneous part and a particular solution:\n\n$$\nx(t)=\\underbrace{{e}^{A(t-{t}_{0})}\\,x({t}_{0})}_{\\text{homogeneous part}}+\\underbrace{{\\int }_{{t}_{0}}^{t}{e}^{A(t-s)}g(s)\\,\\text{d}s}_{\\text{particular solution}},\n$$\n\nwhere $x({t}_{0})$ is the initial condition vector. \n\nLet's evaluate each part separately:\n\n- First, we find the homogeneous part. We are given that ${e}^{At}=[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t) & \\operatorname{sin}⁡(t) \\\\ -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t) & \\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)\\end{aligned}].$ So,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{A(t-1)}\\,x(1) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t-1) & \\operatorname{sin}⁡(t-1) \\\\ -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t-1) & \\operatorname{cos}⁡(t-1)\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t-1)+3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t-1) \\\\ -2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t-1)+3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t-1)\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Next, we find the particular solution. Notice that \n\n$$\n{e}^{A(t-s)}=[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t-s) & \\operatorname{sin}⁡(t-s) \\\\ -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t-s) & \\operatorname{cos}⁡(t-s)\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{A(t-s)}g(s) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t-s) & \\operatorname{sin}⁡(t-s) \\\\ -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t-s) & \\operatorname{cos}⁡(t-s)\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{e}^{s} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{s}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t-s) \\\\ -{e}^{s}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t-s)\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & =[\\begin{aligned}2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t-1)+3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t-1) \\\\ -2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t-1)+3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t-1)\\end{aligned}]+{\\int }_{1}^{t}[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{s}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t-s) \\\\ -{e}^{s}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t-s)\\end{aligned}]\\text{d}s.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186542138 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186073715 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0b6b70eb-df57-5d17-a753-645ccdf94945" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Coordinates of an Endpoint Given the Midpoint"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0f31f1d9-20ae-587f-a12f-646339ce7532" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "11" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541453} {:answer/id #uuid "cafb4598-4e4a-5ef9-8efd-a9be9580151c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541852} {:answer/id #uuid "cd404286-0e08-551f-bf6f-c8bce00a1c38" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541856} {:answer/id #uuid "f32c4e39-cea4-50c5-b6e5-5ec6f145d74d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541935} {:answer/id #uuid "fec1d3b2-ad6a-5e33-a76a-9d8f3aab99ee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541958}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0f31f1d9-20ae-587f-a12f-646339ce7532" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "11" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541453} :answer-field/id #uuid "fae6beff-dfb6-5353-a25e-278027e8d1a6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542105}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "43dcffb9-2ca6-59ab-b26c-63732a3440fb" :question/math-academy-id "q-4101" :question/problem "Find $x+y$ if the midpoint of the line segment with endpoints $(x,y)$ and $(5,-4)$ is $(0,6).$" :question/worked-solution "The given points are $\\underbrace{(x,y)}_{\\text{(}{x}_{1},\\,{y}_{1})}$, $\\underbrace{(5,-4)}_{\\text{(}{x}_{2},\\,{y}_{2})}$ and $\\underbrace{(0,6)}_{\\text{(}{x}_{m},\\,{y}_{m})}.$\n\nFirst, the $x$-coordinate of the midpoint's  must be the average of the $x$-coordinates of the endpoints: \n\n$$\n0=\\frac{x+5}{2}\\,⇒\\,x=-5.\n$$\n\nSimilarly, the $y$-coordinate of the midpoint must be the average of the $y$-coordinates of the endpoints: \n\n$$\n6=\\frac{y+(-4)}{2}\\,⇒\\,y=16.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $(x,y)=(-5,16)$ and $x+y=11.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186544793 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157297 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e93116e7-55c4-5685-8208-a35c627117a3" :knowledge-point/title "Expressing the Total Area Bounded by a Single Polar Curve By Varying the Limits of Integration"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3bdda5ba-c7b8-5b1e-8e9a-e2ebee5f3bc5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}{\\int }_{-3\\pi /8}^{11\\pi /8}{cos}^{2}⁡(2\\theta +\\frac{\\pi }{2})\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544216} {:answer/id #uuid "45b57772-1a24-517a-95cc-031e14b7b047" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{9}{2}{\\int }_{-3\\pi /8}^{9\\pi /8}{cos}^{2}⁡(2\\theta -\\frac{\\pi }{4})\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544240} {:answer/id #uuid "4c27fd42-8cff-578f-b7b7-132d0e72f699" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9{\\int }_{-\\pi /8}^{13\\pi /8}{cos}^{2}⁡(\\theta +\\frac{\\pi }{4})\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544251} {:answer/id #uuid "640c8082-9d89-5f36-b453-20aa27bdcf44" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{9}{2}{\\int }_{-3\\pi /8}^{13\\pi /8}{cos}^{2}⁡(2\\theta +\\frac{\\pi }{4})\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544303} {:answer/id #uuid "ee2ff5b2-7d3f-5bc8-8b07-edf54d53bb3c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{3\\pi /8}^{13\\pi /8}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta +\\frac{\\pi }{4})\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544603}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "640c8082-9d89-5f36-b453-20aa27bdcf44" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{9}{2}{\\int }_{-3\\pi /8}^{13\\pi /8}{cos}^{2}⁡(2\\theta +\\frac{\\pi }{4})\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544303} :answer-field/id #uuid "f3f71d5c-aae5-521c-b9e4-78cdc0efc697" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544773}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "1ca5b3d9-2cf9-5f0b-b261-8040bf2d2224" :question/math-academy-id "q-125695" :question/problem "![](images/17/172737d1a5b28b9a60958dc7e6b1748db1912021b7df4ad26045648063037fd8.png)\n\nThe area of one loop of the polar curve $r=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta +\\frac{\\pi }{4})$ (shown above) is given by the integral\n\n$$\nA=\\frac{9}{2}{\\int }_{-3\\pi /8}^{\\pi /8}{cos}^{2}⁡(2\\theta +\\frac{\\pi }{4})\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nWhich of the following represents the total area bounded by the polar curve?" :question/worked-solution "The given polar curve is the polar rose with $2\\cdot 2=4$ petals, and we know that the area of the first petal is\n\n$$\nA=\\frac{9}{2}{\\int }_{-3\\pi /8}^{\\pi /8}{cos}^{2}⁡(2\\theta +\\frac{\\pi }{4})\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nThe $1$st petal is traversed from ${\\theta }_{1}=-\\frac{3\\pi }{8}$ to ${\\theta }_{2}=\\frac{\\pi }{8},$ and the length of this interval is $\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$\n\nTo find the interval for the second petal, we have to add $\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ to both ends of the interval of the first petal:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\theta }_{3} & ={\\theta }_{1}+\\frac{\\pi }{2}=-\\frac{3\\pi }{8}+\\frac{\\pi }{2}=\\frac{\\pi }{8} \\\\ {\\theta }_{4} & ={\\theta }_{2}+\\frac{\\pi }{2}=\\frac{\\pi }{8}+\\frac{\\pi }{2}=\\frac{5\\pi }{8}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, to obtain the interval for the third petal, we add $\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ to both ends of the interval of the second petal:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\theta }_{5} & ={\\theta }_{3}+\\frac{\\pi }{2}=\\frac{\\pi }{8}+\\frac{\\pi }{2}=\\frac{5\\pi }{8} \\\\ {\\theta }_{6} & ={\\theta }_{4}+\\frac{\\pi }{2}=\\frac{5\\pi }{8}+\\frac{\\pi }{2}=\\frac{9\\pi }{8}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, to obtain the interval for the fourth petal, we add $\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ to both ends of the interval of the third petal:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\theta }_{7} & ={\\theta }_{5}+\\frac{\\pi }{2}=\\frac{5\\pi }{8}+\\frac{\\pi }{2}=\\frac{9\\pi }{8} \\\\ {\\theta }_{8} & ={\\theta }_{6}+\\frac{\\pi }{2}=\\frac{9\\pi }{8}+\\frac{\\pi }{2}=\\frac{13\\pi }{8}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, we obtain the following intervals for all $4$ petals of our rose:\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{[-\\frac{3\\pi }{8},\\frac{\\pi }{8}]}_{1\\text{st}},\\,\\underbrace{[\\frac{\\pi }{8},\\frac{5\\pi }{8}]}_{2\\text{nd}},\\,\\underbrace{[\\frac{5\\pi }{8},\\frac{9\\pi }{8}]}_{3\\text{rd}},\\,\\underbrace{[\\frac{9\\pi }{8},\\frac{13\\pi }{8}]}_{4\\text{th}}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the total area of the given polar curve can be obtained if the curve is traversed from $\\theta =-\\frac{3\\pi }{8}$ to $\\theta =\\frac{13\\pi }{8}:$\n\n$$\n{A}_{\\text{total}}=\\frac{9}{2}{\\int }_{-3\\pi /8}^{13\\pi /8}{cos}^{2}⁡(2\\theta +\\frac{\\pi }{4})\\,\\text{d}\\theta \n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186544813 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157297 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e93116e7-55c4-5685-8208-a35c627117a3" :knowledge-point/title "Expressing the Total Area Bounded by a Single Polar Curve By Varying the Limits of Integration"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6eb18d81-1f63-599d-9330-be80543a661b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4{\\int }_{\\pi /6}^{\\pi /2}{cos}^{2}⁡(3\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544329} {:answer/id #uuid "b2eef91a-4bda-57e9-8dc9-d19c65f2191b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{\\int }_{-\\pi /3}^{2\\pi /3}{cos}^{2}⁡(3\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544465} {:answer/id #uuid "e8c8d26d-0f5e-5aa7-80ac-7d5f0bd84561" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{\\int }_{-\\pi /4}^{5\\pi /4}{cos}^{2}⁡(3\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544590} {:answer/id #uuid "ebc61141-892a-5b47-bb30-ac12b2c0629b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12{\\int }_{-3\\pi /8}^{5\\pi /8}{cos}^{2}⁡(3\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544597} {:answer/id #uuid "f2b98e86-56c1-5a75-b49c-7e744474633d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{\\int }_{-\\pi /6}^{5\\pi /6}{cos}^{2}⁡(3\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544614}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f2b98e86-56c1-5a75-b49c-7e744474633d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{\\int }_{-\\pi /6}^{5\\pi /6}{cos}^{2}⁡(3\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544614} :answer-field/id #uuid "3c945d12-5ddf-5d29-b1ff-6f3e26849c7d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544678}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "43e3bf66-f5d8-57b0-9e72-5dc370f879ec" :question/math-academy-id "q-83484" :question/problem "![](images/34/34e3bbdee3f70fe0111574f0d0fab772acf98de668e2eeb72464e6611a1fabcd.png)\n\nThe area of one loop of the polar curve $r=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3\\theta )$ (shown above) is given by the integral\n\n$$\nA=2{\\int }_{-\\pi /6}^{\\pi /6}{cos}^{2}⁡(3\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nWhich of the following represents the total area bounded by the polar curve?" :question/worked-solution "The given polar curve is the polar rose with $3$ petals, and we know that the area of the first petal is\n\n$$\nA=2{\\int }_{-\\pi /6}^{\\pi /6}{cos}^{2}⁡(3\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\n![](images/34/34e3bbdee3f70fe0111574f0d0fab772acf98de668e2eeb72464e6611a1fabcd.png)\n\nThe $1$st petal is traversed from ${\\theta }_{1}=-\\frac{\\pi }{6}$ to ${\\theta }_{2}=\\frac{\\pi }{6},$ and the length of this interval is $\\frac{\\pi }{3}.$\n\nTo find the interval for the second petal, we have to add $\\frac{\\pi }{3}$ to both ends of the interval of the first petal:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\theta }_{3} & ={\\theta }_{1}+\\frac{\\pi }{3}=-\\frac{\\pi }{6}+\\frac{\\pi }{3}=\\frac{\\pi }{6} \\\\ {\\theta }_{4} & ={\\theta }_{2}+\\frac{\\pi }{3}=\\frac{\\pi }{6}+\\frac{\\pi }{3}=\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, to obtain the interval for the third petal, we add $\\frac{\\pi }{3}$ to both ends of the interval of the second petal:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\theta }_{5} & ={\\theta }_{3}+\\frac{\\pi }{3}=\\frac{\\pi }{6}+\\frac{\\pi }{3}=\\frac{\\pi }{2} \\\\ {\\theta }_{6} & ={\\theta }_{4}+\\frac{\\pi }{3}=\\frac{\\pi }{2}+\\frac{\\pi }{3}=\\frac{5\\pi }{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, we obtain the following intervals for all $3$ petals of our rose:\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{[-\\frac{\\pi }{6},\\frac{\\pi }{6}]}_{1\\text{st}},\\,\\underbrace{[\\frac{\\pi }{6},\\frac{\\pi }{2}]}_{2\\text{nd}},\\,\\underbrace{[\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{5\\pi }{6}]}_{3\\text{rd}}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the total area of the given polar curve can be obtained if the curve is traversed from $\\theta =-\\frac{\\pi }{6}$ to $\\theta =\\frac{5\\pi }{6}:$\n\n$$\n{A}_{\\text{total}}=2{\\int }_{-\\pi /6}^{5\\pi /6}{cos}^{2}⁡(3\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta \n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186544817 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157297 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e93116e7-55c4-5685-8208-a35c627117a3" :knowledge-point/title "Expressing the Total Area Bounded by a Single Polar Curve By Varying the Limits of Integration"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "00e7683d-837d-5f5c-88b4-9f43a2d9f138" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-\\pi /7}^{\\pi /7}{cos}^{2}⁡(7\\theta +\\frac{\\pi }{2})\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544088} {:answer/id #uuid "1747e57c-4ef9-5157-a45d-bc4f36bf748e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-3\\pi /7}^{6\\pi /7}{cos}^{2}⁡(7\\theta -\\frac{\\pi }{2})\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544135} {:answer/id #uuid "4bb3e12e-9656-5b69-bd45-2db95263545f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-\\pi /7}^{\\pi }{cos}^{2}⁡(7\\theta -\\frac{\\pi }{2})\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544249} {:answer/id #uuid "831d59c7-dc3e-55d3-969a-79579b8a2e00" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-\\pi /7}^{6\\pi /7}{cos}^{2}⁡(7\\theta +\\frac{\\pi }{2})\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544373} {:answer/id #uuid "dacb2b57-d53d-5242-94a7-23fcf1973ca7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-\\pi /14}^{3\\pi /7}{cos}^{2}⁡(7\\theta +\\frac{\\pi }{2})\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544559}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "831d59c7-dc3e-55d3-969a-79579b8a2e00" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-\\pi /7}^{6\\pi /7}{cos}^{2}⁡(7\\theta +\\frac{\\pi }{2})\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544373} :answer-field/id #uuid "7cf4a6ed-16c7-5aba-80c0-63cd22eaebcb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544711}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "50affdee-0a40-5255-b196-53de7a7824c6" :question/math-academy-id "q-125863" :question/problem "![](images/66/66616f493d84804ec9c7c2a1e3b8598404f1f57ffda2e0a68718342f508010af.png)\n\nThe area of one loop of the polar curve $r=\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(7\\theta +\\frac{\\pi }{2})$ (shown above) is given by the integral\n\n$$\nA={\\int }_{-\\pi /7}^{0}{cos}^{2}⁡(7\\theta +\\frac{\\pi }{2})\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nWhich of the following represents the total area bounded by the polar curve?" :question/worked-solution "The given polar curve is the polar rose with $7$ petals, and we know that the area of the first petal is\n\n$$\nA={\\int }_{-\\pi /7}^{0}{cos}^{2}⁡(7\\theta +\\frac{\\pi }{2})\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nThe $1$st petal is traversed from ${\\theta }_{1}=-\\frac{\\pi }{7}$ to ${\\theta }_{2}=0,$ and the length of this interval is $\\frac{\\pi }{7}.$\n\nTo find the interval for the second petal, we have to add $\\frac{\\pi }{7}$ to both ends of the interval of the first petal:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\theta }_{3} & ={\\theta }_{1}+\\frac{\\pi }{7}=-\\frac{\\pi }{7}+\\frac{\\pi }{7}=0 \\\\ {\\theta }_{4} & ={\\theta }_{2}+\\frac{\\pi }{7}=0+\\frac{\\pi }{7}=\\frac{\\pi }{7}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, to obtain the interval for the third petal, we add $\\frac{\\pi }{7}$ to both ends of the interval of the second petal:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\theta }_{5} & ={\\theta }_{3}+\\frac{\\pi }{7}=0+\\frac{\\pi }{7}=\\frac{\\pi }{7} \\\\ {\\theta }_{6} & ={\\theta }_{4}+\\frac{\\pi }{7}=\\frac{\\pi }{7}+\\frac{\\pi }{7}=\\frac{2\\pi }{7}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to obtain the interval for the fourth petal, we add $\\frac{\\pi }{7}$ to both ends of the interval of the third petal:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\theta }_{7} & ={\\theta }_{5}+\\frac{\\pi }{7}=\\frac{\\pi }{7}+\\frac{\\pi }{7}=\\frac{2\\pi }{7} \\\\ {\\theta }_{8} & ={\\theta }_{6}+\\frac{\\pi }{7}=\\frac{2\\pi }{7}+\\frac{\\pi }{7}=\\frac{3\\pi }{7}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, to obtain the interval for the fifth petal, we add $\\frac{\\pi }{7}$ to both ends of the interval of the fourth petal:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\theta }_{9} & ={\\theta }_{7}+\\frac{\\pi }{7}=\\frac{2\\pi }{7}+\\frac{\\pi }{7}=\\frac{3\\pi }{7} \\\\ {\\theta }_{10} & ={\\theta }_{8}+\\frac{\\pi }{7}=\\frac{3\\pi }{7}+\\frac{\\pi }{7}=\\frac{4\\pi }{7}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to obtain the interval for the sixth petal, we add $\\frac{\\pi }{7}$ to both ends of the interval of the fifth petal:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\theta }_{11} & ={\\theta }_{9}+\\frac{\\pi }{7}=\\frac{3\\pi }{7}+\\frac{\\pi }{7}=\\frac{4\\pi }{7} \\\\ {\\theta }_{12} & ={\\theta }_{10}+\\frac{\\pi }{7}=\\frac{4\\pi }{7}+\\frac{\\pi }{7}=\\frac{5\\pi }{7}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, to obtain the interval for the seventh petal, we add $\\frac{\\pi }{7}$ to both ends of the interval of the sixth petal:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\theta }_{13} & ={\\theta }_{11}+\\frac{\\pi }{7}=\\frac{4\\pi }{7}+\\frac{\\pi }{7}=\\frac{5\\pi }{7} \\\\ {\\theta }_{14} & ={\\theta }_{12}+\\frac{\\pi }{7}=\\frac{5\\pi }{7}+\\frac{\\pi }{7}=\\frac{6\\pi }{7}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, we obtain the following intervals for all $7$ petals of our rose:\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{[-\\frac{\\pi }{7},0]}_{1\\text{st}},\\,\\underbrace{[0,\\frac{\\pi }{7}]}_{2\\text{nd}},\\,\\underbrace{[\\frac{\\pi }{7},\\frac{2\\pi }{7}]}_{3\\text{rd}},\\,\\underbrace{[\\frac{2\\pi }{7},\\frac{3\\pi }{7}]}_{4\\text{th}},\\,\\underbrace{[\\frac{3\\pi }{7},\\frac{4\\pi }{7}]}_{5\\text{th}}\\,\\underbrace{[\\frac{4\\pi }{7},\\frac{5\\pi }{7}]}_{6\\text{th}},\\,\\underbrace{[\\frac{5\\pi }{7},\\frac{6\\pi }{7}]}_{7\\text{th}}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the total area of the given polar curve can be obtained if the curve is traversed from $\\theta =-\\frac{\\pi }{7}$ to $\\theta =\\frac{6\\pi }{7}:$\n\n$$\n{A}_{\\text{total}}={\\int }_{-\\pi /7}^{6\\pi /7}{cos}^{2}⁡(7\\theta +\\frac{\\pi }{2})\\,\\text{d}\\theta \n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186544843 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157297 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e93116e7-55c4-5685-8208-a35c627117a3" :knowledge-point/title "Expressing the Total Area Bounded by a Single Polar Curve By Varying the Limits of Integration"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0a2cc0dc-e2e1-5b85-87ec-47df41d520a4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{9}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{sin}^{2}⁡(3\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544111} {:answer/id #uuid "3f16e717-df6e-5066-9901-a556dc9ad746" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}{\\int }_{\\pi /3}^{2\\pi /3}{sin}^{2}⁡(3\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544230} {:answer/id #uuid "a55f7afa-35a1-5941-9320-ab16f063a2e6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{9}{2}{\\int }_{\\pi /3}^{2\\pi /3}{sin}^{2}⁡(3\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544444} {:answer/id #uuid "f332fbbe-56f7-5d0e-a468-4f968b7b69ac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{sin}^{2}⁡(3\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544616} {:answer/id #uuid "f8f5048c-ee98-5951-88c7-66b4a1c08544" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi /3}{sin}^{2}⁡(3\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544631}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0a2cc0dc-e2e1-5b85-87ec-47df41d520a4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{9}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{sin}^{2}⁡(3\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544111} :answer-field/id #uuid "e45758eb-810c-5093-acfa-015e3b2ab053" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186544763}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "8b33c7a8-87dc-53ea-9478-54f9a2f3e5e0" :question/math-academy-id "q-83485" :question/problem "![](images/85/85730909004322c2c8b6d065ff1cdb4b0662830007bd5aa14c8c8c4bda798206.png)\n\nThe area of one loop of the polar curve $r=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3\\theta )$ (shown above) is given by the integral\n\n$$\nA=\\frac{9}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}{sin}^{2}⁡(3\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nWhich of the following represents the total area bounded by the polar curve?" :question/worked-solution "The given polar curve is the polar rose with $3$ petals, and we know that the area of the first petal is\n\n$$\nA=\\frac{9}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}{sin}^{2}⁡(3\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\n![](images/85/85730909004322c2c8b6d065ff1cdb4b0662830007bd5aa14c8c8c4bda798206.png)\n\nThe $1$st petal is traversed from ${\\theta }_{1}=0$ to ${\\theta }_{2}=\\frac{\\pi }{3},$ and the length of this interval is $\\frac{\\pi }{3}.$\n\nTo find the interval for the second petal, we have to add $\\frac{\\pi }{3}$ to both ends of the interval of the first petal:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\theta }_{3} & ={\\theta }_{1}+\\frac{\\pi }{3}=0+\\frac{\\pi }{3}=\\frac{\\pi }{3} \\\\ {\\theta }_{4} & ={\\theta }_{2}+\\frac{\\pi }{3}=\\frac{\\pi }{3}+\\frac{\\pi }{3}=\\frac{2\\pi }{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, to obtain the interval for the third petal, we add $\\frac{\\pi }{3}$ to both ends of the interval of the second petal:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\theta }_{5} & ={\\theta }_{3}+\\frac{\\pi }{3}=\\frac{\\pi }{3}+\\frac{\\pi }{3}=\\frac{2\\pi }{3} \\\\ {\\theta }_{6} & ={\\theta }_{4}+\\frac{\\pi }{3}=\\frac{2\\pi }{3}+\\frac{\\pi }{3}=\\pi \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, we obtain the following intervals for all $3$ petals of our rose:\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{[0,\\frac{\\pi }{3}]}_{1\\text{st}},\\,\\underbrace{[\\frac{\\pi }{3},\\frac{2\\pi }{3}]}_{2\\text{nd}},\\,\\underbrace{[\\frac{2\\pi }{3},\\pi ]}_{3\\text{rd}}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the total area of the given polar curve can be obtained if the curve is traversed from $\\theta =0$ to $\\theta =\\pi :$\n\n$$\n{A}_{\\text{total}}=\\frac{9}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{sin}^{2}⁡(3\\theta )\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186545815 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162042 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a6732620-fad1-5dd3-98c5-07ba59d0b286" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the First Factor in the SVD of a Rectangular Matrix: Advanced Cases"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "18d123fb-94f0-5aac-857f-a1c531e7f651" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186545247} {:answer/id #uuid "23d43a08-a125-56be-8714-b272d81f49a4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186545275} {:answer/id #uuid "a716560e-d5d2-5900-8632-2222944452ce" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186545507} {:answer/id #uuid "eea0b1f1-18c6-540d-8e1c-b25479154a86" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186545643}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "eea0b1f1-18c6-540d-8e1c-b25479154a86" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186545643} :answer-field/id #uuid "4003ea6c-85a6-584e-907f-7e4790e055b5" :answer-field/key 